ate-limiter architecture.\n\n3. Caching Strategies (id 85) — cache-aside vs read-through vs\n   write-through vs write-back, the multi-layer hierarchy from\n   browser through CDN to origin, Redis topologies (single,\n   sentinel, cluster), Memcached, CDN edge caching, six invalidation\n   strategies (TTL, explicit, CDC, write-through, surrogate keys,\n   versioned keys), thundering herd / cache stampede with three\n   defences (lock-and-recompute, probabilistic early expiration,\n   stale-while-revalidate), hot keys and hot partitions with four\n   mitigations, eviction policies, multi-region caching, database\n   buffer pools, Kubernetes patterns, and security considerations\n   including web cache deception. Inline SVGs for the access\n   patterns, hierarchy, and stampede defence.\n\n4. Scheduling Systems (id 84) — Kubernetes scheduler internals\n   (filter and score plugins), preemption, topology spread, custom\n   schedulers and the scheduling framework, bin packing heuristics\n   (first-fit, best-fit, FFD), distributed cron with leader leases,\n   Airflow / Nomad / Mesos / per-language job queues, fairness\n   algorithms (DRF), priority classes and quotas, exponential\n   backoff with jitter, idempotency keys, dead letter queues,\n   visibility timeouts, GPU scheduling, and cross-cluster placement\n   with workload identity. Inline SVGs for the scheduler flow and\n   the distributed job queue architecture.\n\nAll 4 posts are wired up automatically via the existing\ngenerate-routes.js + generate-sitemap.js pipelines that read from\nBLOG_POSTS. The Distributed Systems Algorithms post is set as\nfeatured to surface on the home page.\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_0126dj1HZL7BMD66DmDm6DUa\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgrLzsfFISF4by8Q+FKz27YpkK1USsBB+m\namu1QkJnbDsAAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQN1ecN7c\nH57RQ1uevtOk/v3QHVOlxrK8tTabNZflhI1kSPqHkDECZtwhDxxDkx1N2