 whether a Zero\nTrust deployment actually achieves Zero Trust:\n\n1. SPIFFE ID design — flat naming vs hierarchical paths anchored to\n   organizational structure (so authorization can prefix-match instead\n   of enumerating every workload).\n2. Workload attestation — single-selector registration entries that\n   inadvertently grant the SVID to any pod in the namespace.\n3. mTLS bootstrap — long-lived corp-CA-signed certs vs short-lived\n   SVIDs with peer authorization by SPIFFE ID.\n4. Authorization — substring matching vs proper trust-domain +\n   path-prefix checks using the go-spiffe SDK.\n5. Federation — static trust-bundle copies that go stale on rotation\n   vs dynamic bundle endpoints.\n6. Rotation — calling GetX509SVID() once at startup vs the tlsconfig\n   helpers that read the live source on every handshake.\n\nEach scenario:\n- Real YAML/Go config snippet the player has to read\n- Four plausible answer choices with per-choice feedback\n- Production explanation grounded in real SPIFFE/SPIRE behavior\n- Cross-link to the relevant module of the free Mastering SPIFFE &\n  SPIRE course\n\nWires the new route at /games/zero-trust-network-builder, adds the\nsitemap entry and pre-rendered HTML, and flips the hub card from\n\"Coming Soon\" to \"Available\".\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_0126dj1HZL7BMD66DmDm6DUa","tree":{"sha":"c4db43349170ff4d1bd6d68e47ac1c2827d4c776","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vishalanandl177/coderssecret.com/git/trees/c4db43349170ff4d1bd6d68e47ac1c2827d4c776"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vishalanandl177/coderssecret.com/git/commits/1c760ee981e25df2065dbfa1ac17ac983aaa0094","verification":{"payload":"tree c4db43349170ff4d1bd6d68e47ac1c2827d4c776\nparent 683dff4caf11a020fd868b3ed5743174c4a23cb0\nauthor Claude <noreply@anthropic.com> 1777661872 +0000\ncommitter Claude <noreply@anthropic.com> 1777661872 +0000\n\nAdd Zero Trust Network Builder simulator\n\nSecond fully-playable security simulator, focused on workload identity\nwith SPIFFE/SPIRE — the brand''s strongest niche. Six production\nscenarios covering the design decisions that determine whether a Zero\nTrust deployment actually achieves Zero Trust:\n\n1. SPIFFE ID design — flat naming vs hierarchical paths anchored to\n   organizational structure (so authorization can prefix-match instead\n   of enumerating every workload).\n2. Workload attestation — single-selector registration entries that\n   inadvertently grant the SVID to any pod in the namespace.\n3. mTLS bootstrap — long-lived corp-CA-signed certs vs short-lived\n   SVIDs with peer authorization by SPIFFE ID.\n4. Authorization — substring matching vs proper trust-domain +\n   path-prefix checks using the go-spiffe SDK.\n5. Federation — static trust-bundle copies that go stale on rotation\n   vs dynamic bundle endpoints.\n6. Rotation — calling GetX509SVID() once at startup vs the tlsconfig\n   helpers that read the live source on every handshake.\n\nEach scenario:\n- Real YAML/Go config snippet the player has to read\n- Four plausible answer choices with per-choice feedback\n- Production explanation grounded in real SPIFFE/SPIRE behavior\n- Cross-link to the relevant module of the free Mastering SPIFFE &\n  SPIRE course\n\nWires the new route at /games/zero-trust-network-builder, adds the\nsitemap entry and pre-rendered HTML, and flips the hub card from\n\"Coming Soon\" to \"Available\".\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_0126dj1HZL7BMD66DmDm6DUa\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgrLzsfFISF4by8Q+FKz27YpkK1USsBB+m\namu1QkJnbDsAAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQI0sb1oU\nzzhVRKuujQpywMynAK0/HhrEaQ6SDm2fkslaLClBCe5+9gv+p+lsQPRY90Th5TTXVYRfws6xEY8P\nTwc=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-05-01T18:57:58Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/81847?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude/events{/privacy}","fo