"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/ea5edea2c2a91dc13272570f937d38ffa7417b24"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/18b8fc3e1402a2f1dccaf45a13760b6895eefa3e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-22T19:34:47Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-22T19:34:47Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(questdb): correct the WAL worker property and pin the remaining pools (#164)\n\nThe low-power tuning was partly inert. `QDB_CAIRO_WAL_APPLY_WORKER_COUNT`\nis not a QuestDB property — the key is `wal.apply.worker.count`, with no\n`cairo.` prefix, unlike its neighbours `cairo.wal.apply.*`. QuestDB ignores\nan unrecognised QDB_* variable in silence, so the setting looked applied and\nthe pool ran two threads. Verified against 10.0.0: the old spelling reports\n`workers=2` in the startup log, the correct one reports `workers=1`.\n\nThree further pools were never sized. `line.tcp.io.worker.count` was the\ngap that mattered — zero is its default and does NOT fall back to the shared\npool the way it does elsewhere, because ILP keys per-worker state by worker\nid, so it gets a dedicated pool of two whatever the core count. The writer\npool beside it was already pinned to one. The view-compiler, materialised-view\nand live-view pools serve features this plugin never touches and are sized\nand polled regardless.\n\nEach pool also gets a sleep threshold. QuestDB spins 10000 poll cycles\nbefore a worker sleeps, which on a database that is idle most of the time is\na fixed CPU floor rather than a cost that scales with the data. There is no\nglobal default, so a pool left out keeps the full spin.\n\nLogging drops to ERROR. QuestDB records every WAL commit at INFO — several\nlines a second of narration, written to the container log forever. ERROR is\na floor rather than an exact set, so table suspension, ILP parse failures and\nthe max_map_count warning all still appear.\n\nMeasured on this host: 37 worker threads before, 30 after, with 50,000 rows\ningesting normally at one worker per pool. The CPU saving these are aimed at\nis an idle-polling cost that does not reproduce on a fast multi-core x86 box\n— it needs Pi-class hardware to show, and is not claimed here on the strength\nof measurements taken elsewhere.\n\nThe commit mode stays `sync`. Its fsync is what issue #81 exists because of,\nand dropping it would show up as a CPU win while removing the durability a\nboat that loses power actually needs.\n\nTwo tests asserted the misspelled property name and so passed while the\nsetting did nothing; both now assert the real one, and a new test requires\nevery pool given a worker count to be given a sleep threshold too.","tree":{"sha":"de1b42b5299b90e916cbe49ba9bb6357285f45cf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/de1b42b5299b90e916cbe49ba9bb6357285f45cf"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/18b8fc3e1402a2f1dccaf45a13760b6895eefa3e","verification":{"payload":"tree de1b42b5299b90e916cbe49ba9bb6357285f45cf\nparent 830d17b87950568737a30fd251b6487d91239681\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1787427287 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787427287 +1200\n\nfix(questdb): correct the WAL worker property and pin the remaining pools (#164)\n\nThe low-power tuning was partly inert. `QDB_CAIRO_WAL_APPLY_WORKER_COUNT`\nis not a QuestDB property — the key is `wal.apply.worker.count`, with no\n`cairo.` prefix, unlike its neighbours `cairo.wal.apply.*`. QuestDB ignores\nan unrecognised QDB_* variable in silence, so the setting looked applied and\nthe pool ran two threads. Verified against 10.0.0: the old spelling reports\n`workers=2` in the startup log, the correct one reports `workers=1`.\n\nThree further pools were never sized. `line.tcp.io.worker.count` was the\ngap that mattered — zero is its default and does NOT fall back to the shared\npool the way it does elsewhere, because ILP keys per-worker state by worker\nid, so it gets a dedicated pool of two whatever the core count. The writer\npool beside it was already pinned to one. The view-compiler, materialised-view\nand live-view pools serve features this plugin never touches and are sized\nand polled regardless.\n\nEach pool also gets a sleep threshold. QuestDB spins 10000 poll cycles\nbefore a worker sleeps, which on a database that is idle most of the time is\na fixed CPU floor rather than a cost that scales with the data. There is no\nglobal default, so a pool left out keeps the full spin.\n\nLogging drops to ERROR. QuestDB records every WAL commit at INFO — several\nlines a second of narration, written to the container log forever. ERROR is\na floor rather than an exact set, so table suspension, ILP parse failures and\nthe max_map_count warning all still appear.\n\nMeasured on this host: 37 worker threads before, 30 after, with 50,000 rows\ningesting normally at one worker per pool. The CPU saving these are aimed at\nis an idle-polling cost that does not reproduce on a fast multi-core x86 box\n— it needs Pi-class hardware to show, and is not claimed here on the strength\nof measurements taken elsewhere.\n\nThe commit mode stays `sync`. Its fsync is what issue #81 exists because of,\nand dropping it would show up as a CPU win while removing the durability a\nboat that loses power actually needs.\n\nTwo tests asserted the misspelled property name and so passed while the\nsetting did nothing; both now assert the real one, and a new test requires\nevery pool given a worker count to be given a sleep threshold too.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqifnXCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA5qMQAKebKKcan6EIJk5LA92ybD37\nn1z+lLuW2alnOQwG2qmdIYH0evZ/h8r8mbdeN/pjeAXVgaSR+NFS+P/c+VMnrUq6\ntPrCHboLE/sHGz8XPrfDvChszsWs2tQNCQFgipSpuQ0PzZsLykGXp37SfmAxxiOG\nlCUP79xl1rsr5s+xTctIk15YwUSPO/VqqwfzGWQwnrNTy2osrd+OsUh+ywbBDonA\n2z9iumapicH2rjv/pHELlc4VzzqKsDZ4UHQzsHma0D+ORuADg4fNNTeJmU8uKa0U\n8Um9MqaMQLYjbgczTMHJ8OSyw5iLirjXZ14K4K1KbgQK45eaxbnhPCHGu4qNtAj/\nI1g8jym0b/Au6oApiA6gImV+YVM+EvRMyugV+7Xxv6eGANPdca4v1r+hw/jnSzCc\nHx6wzMphz0nRw3vrnc+QW0L00ZeTaSswSPeQWiqOHx9SxYokkBDjY5CmLXw7VpgS\nSMZFCjnCXuTr02Y4UGjmvukm+186Jy3EwgZSoggzKmTK510RXeKfEqJRbMBe5n6k\npgW9/ToV04UI1zHEpBhiuasSKAvV264sMzaZxFMx/u9J+XRO4dEwBPPoF1sYsR/H\nRzqmG+gp7sXwR1gbtBikN2h5vsYqBiL4ZRwoMGOAYfy7k724HI8RZViFjWzwPvcC\nSgTNDg3G4bRvU8m2mboh\n=5tOe\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-22T19:34:48Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow