db/commits/fab300fb23f9d3c06121718eb0d4bbf8f0a8de41"}],"sha":"a49ba47cfdc15e5e641bffd08a23746ba41be5ed","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/a49ba47cfdc15e5e641bffd08a23746ba41be5ed"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/fab300fb23f9d3c06121718eb0d4bbf8f0a8de41/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T20:58:16Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T20:58:16Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(history): support sourcePolicy=all, off by default (#154)\n\n* docs(coderabbit): correct the stated ILP flush interval\n\nThe review config described the ILP writer as flushing \"every 500ms or 1000\nlines\". 500 is the schema MINIMUM for `ilpFlushIntervalMs`, not its default —\n`DEFAULT_FLUSH_INTERVAL_MS` is 5000, ten times larger. The batch size (1000)\nwas right in both places.\n\nIt appears twice, and the second is the one that matters: it sits in the\n\"don''t flag these\" list, so review was being told a wrong constant is a\ndeliberate threshold — misinformation in exactly the section meant to\nsuppress commentary about it.\n\nAlso drops the bare \"picked deliberately\", which was misleading regardless of\nthe number: 5000ms is the default, and the interval is operator-configurable.\n\nSpotted by mairas.\n\n* fix(history): stop the read-path fallbacks degrading in silence\n\nBoth providers read tables whose schema grew over time: `value_kind` and\n`source` arrived in later migrations, and an external QuestDB the plugin does\nnot own may still lack either. The reads degrade rather than fail, which is\nright — hard-failing would turn a read racing ensureTables()''s migration, or a\nlegacy table from 1.x, into user-visible history errors.\n\nWhat was wrong is that degrading said nothing. v1''s execUnified retries\nwithout the column and SUCCEEDS with a different answer: without `value_kind`\na value recorded as a boolean replays as the string \"true\", and without\n`source` every row replays unattributed, so a track fed by two receivers\nzigzags between them with nothing marking why. The session-scoped flags mean\none such retry leaves playback degraded for the rest of the session.\n\nEach degradation is now announced once per state object — not per query — and\nnames the column and what the caller will see instead. The retry itself is\nunchanged.\n\nThe boolean is deliberately still replayed as text: rows written before\n`value_kind` existed carry no record of their original type, so converting\nthem would be guessing. The warning says so rather than leaving it to be\ndiscovered from odd-looking data.\n\nThe two detectors move to src/schema-errors.ts so v2 can use them without a\nthird copy of the regex, and match on a word boundary so `source_id` or\n`sourceRef` cannot be mistaken for the `source` column. Both patterns are\nverified against a live QuestDB.\n\n* feat(history): support sourcePolicy=all, off by default\n\nCloses #139.\n\nsignalk-server #2817 adds `sourcePolicy=all` to History API values requests:\na caller asks for every recording source separated without knowing the\nsourceRefs up front. Each path that does not already name a source expands\ninto one column per source that actually recorded it in range.\n\nOff by default, behind a new plugin setting. Expansion multiplies the work\none request can ask for — a path recorded by four receivers becomes four\nqueries and four columns — which on a Pi-class host turns a cheap request\ninto an expensive one. Both gates must agree: the caller asks with the\npolicy, the operator has to have allowed it.\n\nSource discovery consults all three tables, because a path lives in whichever\none matches its value type and the caller has no way to know which before\nquerying. Probing only the numeric table would expand a boolean channel into\nnothing and silently collapse it back to one merged column. A table that does\nnot exist yet is normal and passes quietly; a missing `source` column warns\nand falls back to one merged column; anything else — a timeout, a 5xx — is\nrethrown rather than reported as \"no sources\", which the caller could not\ntell apart from a genuinely single-source path.\n\nRows whose `source` is unset get their own trailing column, filtered on\n`source IS NULL`. Leaving it unfiltered returns EVERY source''s rows, so that\ncolumn silently duplicated all the others under a \"no source\" label — caught\nby the e2e against a live QuestDB, where it returned 60 rows instead of 20.\n\nA stored sourceRef need not satisfy the identifier guard: \"tcp://gw:2000\" is\nan ordinary Signal K source whose characters the guard rejects, and letting\nit reach the query builder threw and took the WHOLE request down. Such a\nsource is skipped with a warning, and a path whose every source is unusable\nstays one merged column rather than vanishing.\n\nFan-out is capped at 16 columns per path. The sample-bucket cap only applies\nwhen the caller named a resolution, so an unresolved request would otherwise\nschedule one raw query per source with nothing bounding it.\n\nThe sample-bucket cap now counts EXPANDED columns rather than requested\npaths, as #139 called for: counting paths would let a four-receiver path run\nat four times the ceiling the cap exists to enforce.\n\nBREAKING: the v2 values response reports a column''s source as `$source`, not\n`sourceRef`. That is the key #2817 settled on — its own contract test asserts\n`$source` — and it matches what v1 playback already emitted. It applies to\nevery source-bearing column, including the explicit `path|sourceRef` filter\nthat shipped in 2.0.1, so a consumer reading `sourceRef` from a v2 response\nmust be updated. The REQUEST side is unchanged.","tree":{"sha":"23ee32099a48dd694af731b799ccdf26df51a267","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/23ee32099a48dd694af731b799ccdf26df51a267"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/fab300fb23f9d3c06121718eb0d4bbf8f0a8de41","verification":{"payload":"tree 23ee32099a48dd694af731b799ccdf26df51a267\nparent 0eeba81ee7ddb9a4a581b0a85455d53f4eced7ff\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1787173096 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787173096 +1200\n\nfeat(history): support sourcePolicy=all, off by default (#154)\n\n* docs(coderabbit): correct the stated ILP flush interval\n\nThe review config described the ILP writer as flushing \"every 500ms or 1000\nlines\". 500 is the schema MINIMUM for `ilpFlushIntervalMs`, not its default —\n`DEFAULT_FLUSH_INTERVAL_MS` is 5000, ten times larger. The batch size (1000)\nwas right in both places.\n\nIt appears twice, and the second is the one that matters: it sits in the\n\"don''t flag these\" list, so review was being told a wrong constant is a\ndeliberate threshold — misinformation in exactly the section meant to\nsuppress commentary about it.\n\nAlso drops the bare \"picked deliberately\", which was misleading regardless of\nthe number: 5000ms is the default, and the interval is operator-configurable.\n\nSpotted by mairas.\n\n* fix(history): stop the read-path fallbacks degrading in silence\n\nBoth providers read tables whose schema grew over time: `value_kind` and\n`source` arrived in later migrations, and an external QuestDB the plugin does\nnot own may still lack either. The reads degrade rather than fail, which is\nright — hard-failing would turn a read racing ensureTables()''s migration, or a\nlegacy table from 1.x, into user-visible history errors.\n\nWhat was wrong is that degrading said nothing. v1''s execUnified retries\nwithout the column and SUCCEEDS with a different answer: without `value_kind`\na value recorded as a boolean replays as the string \"true\", and without\n`source` every row replays unattributed, so a track fed by two receivers\nzigzags between them with nothing marking why. The session-scoped flags mean\none such retry leaves playback degraded for the rest of the session.\n\nEach degradation is now announced once per state object — not per query — and\nnames the column and what the caller will see instead. The retry itself is\nunchanged.\n\nThe boolean is deliberately still replayed as text: rows written before\n`value_kind` existed carry no record of their original type, so converting\nthem would be guessing. The warning says so rather than leaving it to be\ndiscovered from odd-looking data.\n\nThe two detectors move to src/schema-errors.ts so v2 can use them without a\nthird copy of the regex, and match on a word boundary so `source_id` or\n`sourceRef` cannot be mistaken for the `source` column. Both patterns are\nverified against a live QuestDB.\n\n* feat(history): support sourcePolicy=all, off by default\n\nCloses #139.\n\nsignalk-server #2817 adds `sourcePolicy=all` to History API values requests:\na caller asks for every recording source separated without knowing the\nsourceRefs up front. Each path that does not already name a source expands\ninto one column per source that actually recorded it in range.\n\nOff by default, behind a new plugin setting. Expansion multiplies the work\none request can ask for — a path recorded by four receivers becomes four\nqueries and four columns — which on a Pi-class host turns a cheap request\ninto an expensive one. Both gates must agree: the caller asks with the\npolicy, the operator has to have allowed it.\n\nSource discovery consults all three tables, because a path lives in whichever\none matches its value type and the caller has no way to know which before\nquerying. Probing only the numeric table would expand a boolean channel into\nnothing and silently collapse it back to one merged column. A table that does\nnot exist yet is normal and passes quietly; a missing `source` column warns\nand falls back to one merged column; anything else — a timeout, a 5xx — is\nrethrown rather than reported as \"no sources\", which the caller could not\ntell apart from a genuinely single-source path.\n\nRows whose `source` is unset get their own trailing column, filtered on\n`source IS NULL`. Leaving it unfiltered returns EVERY source''s rows, so that\ncolumn silently duplicated all the others under a \"no source\" label — caught\nby the e2e against a live QuestDB, where it returned 60 rows instead of 20.\n\nA stored sourceRef need not satisfy the identifier guard: \"tcp://gw:2000\" is\nan ordinary Signal K source whose characters the guard rejects, and letting\nit reach the query builder threw and took the WHOLE request down. Such a\nsource is skipped with a warning, and a path whose every source is unusable\nstays one merged column rather than vanishing.\n\nFan-out is capped at 16 columns per path. The sample-bucket cap only applies\nwhen the caller named a resolution, so an unresolved request would otherwise\nschedule one raw query per source with nothing bounding it.\n\nThe sample-bucket cap now counts EXPANDED columns rather than requested\npaths, as #139 called for: counting paths would let a four-receiver path run\nat four times the ceiling the cap exists to enforce.\n\nBREAKING: the v2 values response reports a column''s source as `$source`, not\n`sourceRef`. That is the key #2817 settled on — its own contract test asserts\n`$source` — and it matches what v1 playback already emitted. It applies to\nevery source-bearing column, including the explicit `path|sourceRef` filter\nthat shipped in 2.0.1, so a consumer reading `sourceRef` from a v2 response\nmust be updated. The REQUEST side is unchanged.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqhhjoCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA8JsQAIX4cvsLKHW3iftSlSnEdNSK\nGoFWaWrXpsvEAjvpEyPsJpXjriqsH2CVv+r5CB9V9J3r9bVuXo+F2SLmTP0c7PPp\njrntww6I+Wbi5McF1kVdT9dHEqz6YNiTYp/obvg8fxykPRF1jXnCxHVZ7gn7877P\nE0RNDSCmVl6oHDyIjq0baI+XyQeaB4+qZM1MDejNqMBCcj8M9gc+KoE3PiP1VYtl\n/7tHYgy2hhawLdzWjfLJryc36yKgG2qEouX3GDPHHJzwpkvwlVvYK2glfE1Jtyvw\n/gVBZd2YffAHBSenb1jTgwCHkiq8lUFZtulkmDc67m0mXWF7QGpDgmZc2nXeghpM\noax1uAvEu8eKR4Oub16dUIVlINzlyswhpzHwx2vXd3xzVhhgvRGMnR/FJY+QMEa2\nF0q63MBiuR0eTLhRBos4VbBkQBcF57iCDwwC+WASJHX1NQwq0NPkKVtRGa7TsmKg\nPuy8EzzflG7rD8FYwRNPM2zYKxOonkOxesxCCr9vRyOe7+V0zb/PHclvRGBcIbsz\nWYrkBO8SyCItn6rSPVFcaU7Wc3744rdQ+adZlG2p4HCq88bfdw8wBIFniV54dsKD\nHDWzvL9y2JiIR/DZpmva3rPkOSCJt5SHZ/CGepJp+NmjlaTF2q9pPsCF92GUp5Tb\nqNAvP2OoeYYSZmgoa25h\n=iQ1Y\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-19T20:58:16Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/fab300fb23f9d3c06121718eb0d4bbf8f0a8de41","node_id":"C_kwDOR56yYtoAKGZhYjMwMGZiMjNmOWQzYzA2MTIxNzE4ZWIwZDRiYmY4ZjBhOGRlNDE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/0eeba81ee7ddb9a4a581b0a85455d53f4eced7ff","sha":"0eeba81ee7ddb9a4a581b0a85455d53f4eced7ff","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/0eeba81ee7ddb9a4a581b0a85455d53f4eced7ff"}],"sha":"fab300fb23f9d3c06121718eb0d4bbf8f0a8de41","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/fab300fb23f9d3c06121718eb0d4bbf8f0a8de41"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/0eeba81ee7ddb9a4a581b0a85455d53f4eced7ff/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T17:47:56Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T17:47:56Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(ci): stop the lint step from silently fixing what CI should reject (#153)\n\nThe shared plugin CI runs two lint-ish steps in order: `npm run lint` with\n`continue-on-error: true`, then the blocking `format-check-command` (this\nrepo passes `npm run ci-lint`). Because `lint` was `eslint --fix`, the\nnon-blocking step REPAIRED every auto-fixable violation in the runner''s\ncheckout before the blocking step looked at the code — so the blocking\nstep found nothing, and the fix was thrown away with the runner.\n\nThe effect is that auto-fixable errors never fail CI: prefer-const, unused\nimports that carry a fixer, quote and semicolon rules. Reproduced by adding\na `let` that is never reassigned — `eslint` flags it, `eslint --fix`\nrewrites it to `const` and exits clean, and the blocking check then passes\non code that was never actually correct in the repo.\n\n`lint` is now a plain `eslint`, so the CI step reports honestly and the\nblocking check sees the same violations a developer does. Fixing moves to\n`lint:fix`, which `format` now calls — so `npm run format` behaves exactly\nas before for local use.","tree":{"sha":"7d1503c78e5315661c3b400bf3a211d99c0c98e7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/7d1503c78e5315661c3b400bf3a211d99c0c98e7"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/0eeba81ee7ddb9a4a581b0a85455d53f4eced7ff","verification":{"payload":"tree 7d1503c78e5315661c3b400bf3a211d99c0c98e7\nparent 42c5772be6b1e4407bd087b377d37724f4166e4f\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1787161676 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787161676 +1200\n\nfix(ci): stop the lint step from silently fixing what CI should reject (#153)\n\nThe shared plugin CI runs two lint-ish steps in order: `npm run lint` with\n`continue-on-error: true`, then the blocking `format-check-command` (this\nrepo passes `npm run ci-lint`). Because `lint` was `eslint --fix`, the\nnon-blocking step REPAIRED every auto-fixable violation in the runner''s\ncheckout before the blocking step looked at the code — so the blocking\nstep found nothing, and the fix was thrown away with the runner.\n\nThe effect is that auto-fixable errors never fail CI: prefer-const, unused\nimports that carry a fixer, quote and semicolon rules. Reproduced by adding\na `let` that is never reassigned — `eslint` flags it, `eslint --fix`\nrewrites it to `const` and exits clean, and the blocking check then passes\non code that was never actually correct in the repo.\n\n`lint` is now a plain `eslint`, so the CI step reports honestly and the\nblocking check sees the same violations a developer does. Fixing moves to\n`lint:fix`, which `format` now calls — so `npm run format` behaves exactly\nas before for local use.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqhexMCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAM+4QAILZ1/TzIBHE9QK12O32z03H\n11ALwPTqJhzSVEhyHc+vxyG7Z0bqhD4z40bM/3qJHcKzsi4IAK3VgBciMb0aiq6C\nCs7ocoZWoibUy9+fBhZFnk4Enfv02d0c6OUXvm810XQtbkRHlWt1228jneMryi9m\nmHpREdvbpIKMabD7hp/W9iyfICpZ/Oc8rJesq46tlR8+wNoPDiTQKgi3W6W4uIMo\nLeWaQmWDDPyI2UasuGVdZ77ak9rXE3IgoJdvl7X0GJaPt0w2skavxyVxarrc7+Mw\no1l50eb3JTyxhiH6IWFAqe6qnJ5GRq34s06VKhVF81oTCDlHkeJ27nzsTXavQT4P\nOWONkRVQVbWBdVTj+NQrsRkZwJZfkSyCE30xAOtmtFV+IGX2xLm0mRT4nwsqbb78\nwCHEBeludFkUu4wptG86OJjiHrsKCGxTaXRsrcWBO8UDLOvuWe4MfKXzUb0dj63B\nwT6+XHp9ZMpLFvNy6tL8xMBRxCq6VTOFuyvd61zOrxomaU6O7pksO6VJRy4HNvoG\nDCkBRcC/QcfX6AxWDtrusmyD2iqq+sCZHDpnYnGn0Gcrtios2XnXR8HUGFvakc/3\ncCrpWp+KV2bnByvCpJko5MOP8AON7pTTnZOCgql5j2CfMpRYZtbTXKSw0fR3XEgS\nsYloU8Nn28NJHpFYDXj/\n=uK0k\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-19T17:47:57Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/0eeba81ee7ddb9a4a581b0a85455d53f4eced7ff","node_id":"C_kwDOR56yYtoAKDBlZWJhODFlZTdkZGI5YTRhNTgxYjBhODU0NTVkNTNmNGVjZWQ3ZmY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/42c5772be6b1e4407bd087b377d37724f4166e4f","sha":"42c5772be6b1e4407bd087b377d37724f4166e4f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/42c5772be6b1e4407bd087b377d37724f4166e4f"}],"sha":"0eeba81ee7ddb9a4a581b0a85455d53f4eced7ff","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/0eeba81ee7ddb9a4a581b0a85455d53f4eced7ff"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/42c5772be6b1e4407bd087b377d37724f4166e4f/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T03:19:09Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T03:19:09Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: import history from InfluxDB into QuestDB (#150)\n\nThe InfluxDB Migration panel could detect an InfluxDB and then do nothing\nwith it — there was no endpoint behind the detected instances, so the\ncards were inert and a user asking \"what am I missing?\" was missing\nnothing. This adds the import the panel was already advertising.\n\nSupports both majors, which share no reading code: 2.x over Flux and 1.x\nover InfluxQL. Discovery lists buckets/databases and their measurements,\nthen an import runs in the background over a chosen time range while the\npanel polls progress and can cancel it.\n\nHow source data maps onto the three tables:\n\n  - A measurement with the conventional `value` field becomes the Signal K\n    path of the same name; several named fields become `measurement.field`\n    paths so two fields cannot overwrite each other.\n  - Numbers go to `signalk`, strings and booleans to `signalk_str`\n    (booleans tagged value_kind=boolean), and latitude/longitude field\n    pairs are recombined into `signalk_position`. A half pair is skipped\n    rather than written as a bare number.\n  - Values are classified by what they actually are, reusing the live\n    path''s routeDeltaValue, so imported data lands exactly where recorded\n    data would.\n\nRows keep their ORIGINAL nanosecond timestamps. The existing write() path\ncould not be reused for this: it stamps rows from the server clock and\nratchets a monotonic counter, and its explicit-Date branch truncates to\nmilliseconds. Since the tables dedup on KEYS(ts, path, context, source),\ntwo source points 200µs apart would have collided and the later would\nhave silently upserted over the earlier. The new *AtNanos writers take the\nsource timestamp verbatim and deliberately leave the live writer''s floor\nalone.\n\nThat same dedup key makes the import idempotent: re-running a range\noverwrites rather than duplicating, so an interrupted import is simply\nre-run. Every imported row carries source=influxdb-import.\n\nReads are windowed a day at a time and apply backpressure against the ILP\nbuffer — without it a fast InfluxDB outruns the socket and the buffer cap\ndiscards its OLDEST lines, silently losing the very history being copied.\nAnything unmappable is counted as skipped rather than dropped quietly.\n\nCredentials are request-scoped and never written to the plugin settings.\nA run is cancelled on plugin stop, so it cannot outlive the writer it\nholds. Only one import runs at a time.","tree":{"sha":"285525e82ede0affaa774024107c58f1e9a556ee","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/285525e82ede0affaa774024107c58f1e9a556ee"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/42c5772be6b1e4407bd087b377d37724f4166e4f","verification":{"payload":"tree 285525e82ede0affaa774024107c58f1e9a556ee\nparent 8fdf144dceb0d39842763cfdfa7b0cab330fc5ea\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1787109549 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787109549 +1200\n\nfeat: import history from InfluxDB into QuestDB (#150)\n\nThe InfluxDB Migration panel could detect an InfluxDB and then do nothing\nwith it — there was no endpoint behind the detected instances, so the\ncards were inert and a user asking \"what am I missing?\" was missing\nnothing. This adds the import the panel was already advertising.\n\nSupports both majors, which share no reading code: 2.x over Flux and 1.x\nover InfluxQL. Discovery lists buckets/databases and their measurements,\nthen an import runs in the background over a chosen time range while the\npanel polls progress and can cancel it.\n\nHow source data maps onto the three tables:\n\n  - A measurement with the conventional `value` field becomes the Signal K\n    path of the same name; several named fields become `measurement.field`\n    paths so two fields cannot overwrite each other.\n  - Numbers go to `signalk`, strings and booleans to `signalk_str`\n    (booleans tagged value_kind=boolean), and latitude/longitude field\n    pairs are recombined into `signalk_position`. A half pair is skipped\n    rather than written as a bare number.\n  - Values are classified by what they actually are, reusing the live\n    path''s routeDeltaValue, so imported data lands exactly where recorded\n    data would.\n\nRows keep their ORIGINAL nanosecond timestamps. The existing write() path\ncould not be reused for this: it stamps rows from the server clock and\nratchets a monotonic counter, and its explicit-Date branch truncates to\nmilliseconds. Since the tables dedup on KEYS(ts, path, context, source),\ntwo source points 200µs apart would have collided and the later would\nhave silently upserted over the earlier. The new *AtNanos writers take the\nsource timestamp verbatim and deliberately leave the live writer''s floor\nalone.\n\nThat same dedup key makes the import idempotent: re-running a range\noverwrites rather than duplicating, so an interrupted import is simply\nre-run. Every imported row carries source=influxdb-import.\n\nReads are windowed a day at a time and apply backpressure against the ILP\nbuffer — without it a fast InfluxDB outruns the socket and the buffer cap\ndiscards its OLDEST lines, silently losing the very history being copied.\nAnything unmappable is counted as skipped rather than dropped quietly.\n\nCredentials are request-scoped and never written to the plugin settings.\nA run is cancelled on plugin stop, so it cannot outlive the writer it\nholds. Only one import runs at a time.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqhSCtCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAASxIQAKasySMFLA+oARDOL46m2Qq4\nlR0SxmxBpZGMZqQ5qa8R2yeCYj26aTGEj5+2zWyUK9/zMrOa33+y0BMn5iP8h9Io\nTuF8cvHPLtKcPrijHrJk9p+XJAtcGBCMAAhtXczOYL+m42PO4Ohn6dJgJgXI/o/I\nKuodjhPp48/24PIFpJf1Nk7aXWCO2lpSNS9prWYJiebP+EuEEYvEui1cHX7lOwu1\nki10bqd2c2g4rcWu1SSWAMfPyY9vNAUHxIAg5BCdGPst9MWtrANGJpxsg0kBTcmG\no/0nIZy5gtELU8Gk6FXLomu541rZnKjqEaOveNF+svaZBVy00q4mJK/eNTKH3CVM\nUBS9+PddbIIemy6FnEybZWvozLTEQuWn6sCIM0RFOsSIVacX5WUdPa9HLu+kcnV6\nJeKtyoEFvh80/GgEK/I7lizKkUCw7soUxtdz8YUaxqkWYHgospFU4t98AP2pfFeE\n3RYlXoKCmttPrReheb/VlecJ9lZI39E0oUdIGkp5PoEgfCnvEt7gzgWahkZvW0bq\nYsdytGdCEP3JVkYokayUWosDLwfa9uuEiSn/5IbkSCjynPNf5QSNksscPxSDjWlj\nH+fxwW6tORIzdVSDqakDqNa1i7f2BCqoHcW57T5Ihjm/rp2X9iNV70ndHK87mRgf\nlmrBKwkgw7PvGGFtatyT\n=8jg6\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-19T03:19:09Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/42c5772be6b1e4407bd087b377d37724f4166e4f","node_id":"C_kwDOR56yYtoAKDQyYzU3NzJiZTZiMWU0NDA3YmQwODdiMzc3ZDM3NzI0ZjQxNjZlNGY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/8fdf144dceb0d39842763cfdfa7b0cab330fc5ea","sha":"8fdf144dceb0d39842763cfdfa7b0cab330fc5ea","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/8fdf144dceb0d39842763cfdfa7b0cab330fc5ea"}],"sha":"42c5772be6b1e4407bd087b377d37724f4166e4f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/42c5772be6b1e4407bd087b377d37724f4166e4f"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/8fdf144dceb0d39842763cfdfa7b0cab330fc5ea/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T02:46:57Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T02:46:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(restore): replay AIS ship type so restored targets keep their colour (#152)\n\n* fix(restore): replay AIS ship type so restored targets keep their colour\n\ndesign.aisShipType is an object {id, name} that the plugin records as\nflattened leaves (design.aisShipType.id in the numeric table,\ndesign.aisShipType.name in the string table — issue #128). The identity\nrestore set listed only the bare path, which has no rows, so ship type was\nnever replayed: every restored AIS target came back with the default colour\nbecause Freeboard colours by aisShipType.id (issue #148). Same object-vs-leaf\ngap already fixed for design.length; aisShipType was missed.\n\nAdd both leaves to IDENTITY_PATHS (keeping the bare path for pre-flattening\ndatabases), so they restore over the 24h identity window alongside the name.\nOn a live install this reaches 116 vessels that previously restored none.\n\n- src/restore.ts: aisShipType.id / .name added to IDENTITY_PATHS\n- test: a flattened ship type restores over the identity window\n- README: name the ship-type restore and the colour it brings back\n\n* test(restore): cover the id-only ship-type case (the colour payload)\n\nSelf-audit gap: the existing test always supplied both aisShipType leaves,\nbut the fix''s actual payload is the .id leaf alone (Freeboard colours by id;\nthe name is cosmetic). Add a test that restores a target coloured from .id\nwith no .name present.","tree":{"sha":"4b8a819e3068fc5befacf0b4972cce84d3c9a861","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/4b8a819e3068fc5befacf0b4972cce84d3c9a861"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/8fdf144dceb0d39842763cfdfa7b0cab330fc5ea","verification":{"payload":"tree 4b8a819e3068fc5befacf0b4972cce84d3c9a861\nparent f8586ad64c0782d64666ca926f3f7392a1ae7c8a\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1787107617 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787107617 +1200\n\nfix(restore): replay AIS ship type so restored targets keep their colour (#152)\n\n* fix(restore): replay AIS ship type so restored targets keep their colour\n\ndesign.aisShipType is an object {id, name} that the plugin records as\nflattened leaves (design.aisShipType.id in the numeric table,\ndesign.aisShipType.name in the string table — issue #128). The identity\nrestore set listed only the bare path, which has no rows, so ship type was\nnever replayed: every restored AIS target came back with the default colour\nbecause Freeboard colours by aisShipType.id (issue #148). Same object-vs-leaf\ngap already fixed for design.length; aisShipType was missed.\n\nAdd both leaves to IDENTITY_PATHS (keeping the bare path for pre-flattening\ndatabases), so they restore over the 24h identity window alongside the name.\nOn a live install this reaches 116 vessels that previously restored none.\n\n- src/restore.ts: aisShipType.id / .name added to IDENTITY_PATHS\n- test: a flattened ship type restores over the identity window\n- README: name the ship-type restore and the colour it brings back\n\n* test(restore): cover the id-only ship-type case (the colour payload)\n\nSelf-audit gap: the existing test always supplied both aisShipType leaves,\nbut the fix''s actual payload is the .id leaf alone (Freeboard colours by id;\nthe name is cosmetic). Add a test that restores a target coloured from .id\nwith no .name present.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqhRkhCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAPzEQAA4htCvcnRkXCGuS5m63Ef27\nhT8XV9Q06HbQEpJup+q0H/fWV5FWfPRydZXz9/VkvUv4cyi4jPtrW5c9IJf6tk5s\n4QXxqegZzEqN3LE+t1DGu11YjC+zfpBIrcD997nSDkLWfNgEPs0W62IQ2EmspD4k\nx2AMB/NNm3JCXnB2ZmP900600wWOhW/XzUtDb4HfCD7GhSh1KsNvE55gNZ+7Lrun\n4SvWm1hTaWa8BqcwNXnDWzaBPPA9eLYYJw2zdn0+VOvbnU4b4pl/XkUohfzbyiCp\nkroRPzzeWpxXs4qXmfxrj3pTxhGWvzf3B+VhWx4Yr1xHk/TDMUFJ3a5Ui7qCup04\nV0zsIxQEKLV1meKydl7x8J2RdBocyVWIP8rWO9yRJmw1fGRkh2nXFDozTnOgiHzj\n43R5nO+xxE3Bb7e6ztNFyUWOAAvkfXt05/KilY/l1V8hr3N5BRkFRM7fgWrP4EV2\nHqJt68Z8nZ59B39DS7WQqX68IDiSg+e8p4XUWZdkMX/V2LJLMWgXEdPlqLDkenYA\nT2AsIhpbIjtfN2MCWkorWYJXyVLtsKaG31EC4gGw9j4oLnSwGPxgsIYFea3r+DDk\ni4HWGSwuM9fcOoqNdrreoN1Joo2XZt7E/DdO8Mvt1EL2lwn/X8LFQNUepFRJeQVO\nctyiRMpE08VgykjQPPPQ\n=coEM\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-19T02:46:57Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/8fdf144dceb0d39842763cfdfa7b0cab330fc5ea","node_id":"C_kwDOR56yYtoAKDhmZGYxNDRkY2ViMGQzOTg0Mjc2M2NmZGZhN2IwY2FiMzMwZmM1ZWE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/f8586ad64c0782d64666ca926f3f7392a1ae7c8a","sha":"f8586ad64c0782d64666ca926f3f7392a1ae7c8a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/f8586ad64c0782d64666ca926f3f7392a1ae7c8a"}],"sha":"8fdf144dceb0d39842763cfdfa7b0cab330fc5ea","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/8fdf144dceb0d39842763cfdfa7b0cab330fc5ea"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/f8586ad64c0782d64666ca926f3f7392a1ae7c8a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-18T22:54:50Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-18T22:54:50Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: surface a wedged QuestDB container as a plugin error (#151)\n\n* feat: surface a wedged QuestDB container as a plugin error\n\nWhen signalk-container defers a drift recreate because it can''t stop or\nremove the container (orphaned rootless-Podman user namespace after a\nPodman service restart, or a permission error), it fires the new\nonContainerWedged advisory once. Wire it at both ensureRunning call sites\nto app.setPluginError so the operator sees the recovery remedy in the\nadmin UI instead of only a silent per-reconcile drift loop in the server\nlog. On signalk-container < 1.29.0 the callback never fires — no-op.\n\nThe advisory defers rather than throws, so the start path keeps running\nto the \"Recording…\" status line. Both post-ensureRunning status writes\n(initial start and version update) are now guarded by pluginErrorActive\nso a wedge error set inside ensureRunning is not immediately clobbered by\na false \"Recording\" (which on the update path would also wrongly imply\nthe new tag was applied).\n\nBumps signalk-container-helper to ^0.7.0 for the ContainerWedged type.\n\n* fix: keep the wedged-container error visible and gate update success on it\n\nCodeRabbit review of #151, plus a copy-paste bug of my own:\n\n- Remove a duplicated comment block above publishRecordingStatus.\n- The startup progress lines (\"Waiting…\", \"Creating tables…\") run after\n  ensureRunning and overwrote the error onContainerWedged set (it defers,\n  it does not throw), so guarding only the final recording write wasn''t\n  enough. Track the wedge in a `containerWedged` state var (cleared at each\n  ensureRunning attempt) and re-assert publishError at the end of startup so\n  the operator''s final status is the remedy, not a stale progress line.\n- /api/update/apply persisted questdbVersion=newTag BEFORE ensureRunning and\n  always returned {status:\"updated\"} — so a wedged update (old container\n  still running) claimed success and persisted a version that never applied,\n  surviving restarts. Move the savePluginOptions persist to AFTER a confirmed\n  recreate and gate it (and the success response) on !containerWedged; when\n  wedged, return 409 with the wedge remedy instead.","tree":{"sha":"413761474f22fe875a67d31fd51aa41c65dbae64","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/413761474f22fe875a67d31fd51aa41c65dbae64"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/f8586ad64c0782d64666ca926f3f7392a1ae7c8a","verification":{"payload":"tree 413761474f22fe875a67d31fd51aa41c65dbae64\nparent 87f7d39c0b84a10fcfebbc020a8b71f23551d25c\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1787093690 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787093690 +1200\n\nfeat: surface a wedged QuestDB container as a plugin error (#151)\n\n* feat: surface a wedged QuestDB container as a plugin error\n\nWhen signalk-container defers a drift recreate because it can''t stop or\nremove the container (orphaned rootless-Podman user namespace after a\nPodman service restart, or a permission error), it fires the new\nonContainerWedged advisory once. Wire it at both ensureRunning call sites\nto app.setPluginError so the operator sees the recovery remedy in the\nadmin UI instead of only a silent per-reconcile drift loop in the server\nlog. On signalk-container < 1.29.0 the callback never fires — no-op.\n\nThe advisory defers rather than throws, so the start path keeps running\nto the \"Recording…\" status line. Both post-ensureRunning status writes\n(initial start and version update) are now guarded by pluginErrorActive\nso a wedge error set inside ensureRunning is not immediately clobbered by\na false \"Recording\" (which on the update path would also wrongly imply\nthe new tag was applied).\n\nBumps signalk-container-helper to ^0.7.0 for the ContainerWedged type.\n\n* fix: keep the wedged-container error visible and gate update success on it\n\nCodeRabbit review of #151, plus a copy-paste bug of my own:\n\n- Remove a duplicated comment block above publishRecordingStatus.\n- The startup progress lines (\"Waiting…\", \"Creating tables…\") run after\n  ensureRunning and overwrote the error onContainerWedged set (it defers,\n  it does not throw), so guarding only the final recording write wasn''t\n  enough. Track the wedge in a `containerWedged` state var (cleared at each\n  ensureRunning attempt) and re-assert publishError at the end of startup so\n  the operator''s final status is the remedy, not a stale progress line.\n- /api/update/apply persisted questdbVersion=newTag BEFORE ensureRunning and\n  always returned {status:\"updated\"} — so a wedged update (old container\n  still running) claimed success and persisted a version that never applied,\n  surviving restarts. Move the savePluginOptions persist to AFTER a confirmed\n  recreate and gate it (and the success response) on !containerWedged; when\n  wedged, return 409 with the wedge remedy instead.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqhOK6CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAW04QAJnR7KcBiWmlTZ2KNNl69Dfz\nkPLPmIain2d1qVBUmMgC8Ch89pMu4UEJ/4ZNm0t5lT+PzUjv2dv3xebGK6gEmAXw\nyF4dJGPd9zM4zlwOrVFs+fgB88B544amVm3fr1eh6aJgr/RoYw6VAoToh6uHmaSv\nUICCpL2uVpRAPSRYBdVrHQgFNQGHKvou5a7JGDs2RB2NDzCCfLRD8Ebgj6/HF590\nXi4qQHyv6xDryOds/kTfsnmg49I1WPRNs5yw+9TdaZq1oxSCcBzGsq287TzlAndL\nvi/0pI5SxPcKI6olJ6x9wFoXv9MsaEqc6S4Zz5QAcmgCJ/VZG1Zm1UcUDBsKhXie\nrjNDa9UMU9H56xvGRubl3sJo9JpSqQROlXLEdTcEAdXdJMeUgzRF81eTbrjHFMlj\nasDM5khM25BIPmWoKHwf4FGGsEhH2wovCxmtBW1mL7uu06GwnMan1RTKUui+540u\nT/unEwSZHhDPP1G+NZZYoc3pHaOqqKNWLW4bvNKCm2m9+e+R9CdqMFphCZ3znXNI\nNq/0EZwpPi5thrNwEMaETw6AUO0TxcugxouBNQTwrz4LHVb/Enxh5YCqDikRLm9c\nyo/6zFoHk9sI3bSBRkCZAihF71ZJaDYnPWtK7mkffX4K6op5PKoe5cvCPL3+NXtC\n1Pnz2UvL3YScRLAYkvqo\n=Udm0\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-18T22:54:51Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/f8586ad64c0782d64666ca926f3f7392a1ae7c8a","node_id":"C_kwDOR56yYtoAKGY4NTg2YWQ2NGMwNzgyZDY0NjY2Y2E5MjZmM2Y3MzkyYTFhZTdjOGE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/87f7d39c0b84a10fcfebbc020a8b71f23551d25c","sha":"87f7d39c0b84a10fcfebbc020a8b71f23551d25c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/87f7d39c0b84a10fcfebbc020a8b71f23551d25c"}],"sha":"f8586ad64c0782d64666ca926f3f7392a1ae7c8a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/f8586ad64c0782d64666ca926f3f7392a1ae7c8a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/87f7d39c0b84a10fcfebbc020a8b71f23551d25c/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-18T21:41:03Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-18T21:41:03Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: report one InfluxDB server once, with the right major version (#149)\n\n* fix: report one InfluxDB server once, with the right major version\n\nDetection probed `HEAD /ping` and `GET /health` independently and pushed a\nsource for each hit. Both endpoints answer on both majors, so a single\nserver matched twice: a v2.9.1 instance showed up in the config panel as\n\"InfluxDB 1.x found\" AND \"InfluxDB 2.x found\", pointing at the same URL.\n\nVerified against live containers:\n\n  - influxdb:latest (2.9.1) serves the 1.x-compat /ping and answers 204.\n  - influxdb:1.8 (1.8.10) serves /health and answers {\"status\":\"pass\"}.\n\nNeither endpoint identifies the major version, so the reported version\nstring now decides it and each server is reported exactly once. That also\nfixes the misclassification the other way: a 1.8 server was only ever\nlabelled 1.x because /health happened to be probed second.\n\nThe version is also stripped of its leading \"v\" — InfluxDB reports\n\"v2.9.1\" and the panel renders \"v{version}\", which read as \"vv2.9.1\".\n\nDetection and URL validation move to src/influx-detect.ts so both are\nunit-testable without a router; the URL guard additionally rejects\nnon-http(s) schemes, which the inline version accepted.\n\n* fix: repair three detection gaps found while auditing\n\nAll three are in the module this branch introduces, so they are folded in\nhere rather than chased in a follow-up.\n\nA URL carrying credentials was accepted and then failed to probe at all.\nNode''s fetch REFUSES such a URL (\"Request cannot be constructed from a URL\nthat includes credentials\") and the resulting TypeError is swallowed by the\nprobe''s catch, so pasting `http://user:pw@host:8086` reported \"no InfluxDB\nfound\" against a perfectly healthy server, with nothing saying why. The\ncredentials are now stripped during normalisation, alongside the query\nstring and fragment. InfluxDB auth belongs in the token / username fields,\nwhich is where the import already reads it from. Verified against a live\n2.9.1: the same URL that previously found nothing now detects it.\n\nDetection also conflated \"nothing answered\" with \"answered without naming a\nversion\", because both were carried as `undefined` in one variable. A server\nwhose /health returns {\"status\":\"pass\"} with no `version` field — a reverse\nproxy or auth gateway can strip it — and whose /ping is disabled was\nreported as NOT RUNNING. The two facts are now tracked separately, so such a\nserver is found with version \"unknown\"; /ping is still consulted for the\nversion header, since that is what distinguishes 1.x from 2.x.\n\nFinally, the 3.x exclusion matched `3\\.` and so missed a version reported as\nexactly \"3\" (or \"3-alpha\"), which then fell through to the 1.x label — the\nvery misidentification this module exists to prevent. The major is now\nmatched with an explicit boundary.\n\nEach new test fails against the previous commit, one per defect.","tree":{"sha":"bd17b25c05c4afc129e24bbb5a587575ed5bc714","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/bd17b25c05c4afc129e24bbb5a587575ed5bc714"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/87f7d39c0b84a10fcfebbc020a8b71f23551d25c","verification":{"payload":"tree bd17b25c05c4afc129e24bbb5a587575ed5bc714\nparent 8340bb03625325d88264874f3b9a20e75c22415b\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1787089263 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787089263 +1200\n\nfix: report one InfluxDB server once, with the right major version (#149)\n\n* fix: report one InfluxDB server once, with the right major version\n\nDetection probed `HEAD /ping` and `GET /health` independently and pushed a\nsource for each hit. Both endpoints answer on both majors, so a single\nserver matched twice: a v2.9.1 instance showed up in the config panel as\n\"InfluxDB 1.x found\" AND \"InfluxDB 2.x found\", pointing at the same URL.\n\nVerified against live containers:\n\n  - influxdb:latest (2.9.1) serves the 1.x-compat /ping and answers 204.\n  - influxdb:1.8 (1.8.10) serves /health and answers {\"status\":\"pass\"}.\n\nNeither endpoint identifies the major version, so the reported version\nstring now decides it and each server is reported exactly once. That also\nfixes the misclassification the other way: a 1.8 server was only ever\nlabelled 1.x because /health happened to be probed second.\n\nThe version is also stripped of its leading \"v\" — InfluxDB reports\n\"v2.9.1\" and the panel renders \"v{version}\", which read as \"vv2.9.1\".\n\nDetection and URL validation move to src/influx-detect.ts so both are\nunit-testable without a router; the URL guard additionally rejects\nnon-http(s) schemes, which the inline version accepted.\n\n* fix: repair three detection gaps found while auditing\n\nAll three are in the module this branch introduces, so they are folded in\nhere rather than chased in a follow-up.\n\nA URL carrying credentials was accepted and then failed to probe at all.\nNode''s fetch REFUSES such a URL (\"Request cannot be constructed from a URL\nthat includes credentials\") and the resulting TypeError is swallowed by the\nprobe''s catch, so pasting `http://user:pw@host:8086` reported \"no InfluxDB\nfound\" against a perfectly healthy server, with nothing saying why. The\ncredentials are now stripped during normalisation, alongside the query\nstring and fragment. InfluxDB auth belongs in the token / username fields,\nwhich is where the import already reads it from. Verified against a live\n2.9.1: the same URL that previously found nothing now detects it.\n\nDetection also conflated \"nothing answered\" with \"answered without naming a\nversion\", because both were carried as `undefined` in one variable. A server\nwhose /health returns {\"status\":\"pass\"} with no `version` field — a reverse\nproxy or auth gateway can strip it — and whose /ping is disabled was\nreported as NOT RUNNING. The two facts are now tracked separately, so such a\nserver is found with version \"unknown\"; /ping is still consulted for the\nversion header, since that is what distinguishes 1.x from 2.x.\n\nFinally, the 3.x exclusion matched `3\\.` and so missed a version reported as\nexactly \"3\" (or \"3-alpha\"), which then fell through to the 1.x label — the\nvery misidentification this module exists to prevent. The major is now\nmatched with an explicit boundary.\n\nEach new test fails against the previous commit, one per defect.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqhNFvCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAADk8QAICwV8FzYUFURyW2yHRhwrwV\n0MmISaW88vX3J9p7xQlU89g+P5KHMZXTYRjeB8onwp+bF3DS9sX0T0Zoh2z9X/gE\nOA9nm7NuctQfhWVA2wRuxwACrtd0lZhdbxett0c5f0LWDQ9HOhHBRLarpoDuMxj7\n/YQZv4IN1G1XpefIPrzg7aQT7e4V3x45C1XhMdxOrVJS6oTDrC3IAsWDXwYfKAIS\nHd1+0znSqbgRUCffMtJZgpZAeWzT+6K+UfWlViVXQnyJAb3pwOLSSMeKvQkXJV3Y\na6q6OszInAZoyQAkSQhf+v6UITd/j/tXWDJpT2Pt1tY5P+XivoxliQsBgCpXmi/2\nGI8MGITV0Keo+P/wW3+44wzrEORLjMCcD9U1RN8qMHRVo9tI3bpb7axmicHBTM7D\nsoD+Hf2a9dMi5chGlC+bLRLQ5HThbf2etoV7kNcJ/PpxZaiEkK+NynjTfb/CPL9u\nY9jDjFMX51Mfq1n5sS3zE6WTYlP7/gX//UnfMzcKOAv2u1D+pA5rMjyjuDHbLHr7\nhN9uexJ8UKFCvOcLPqZ566wXIq38CAezAE200iqScwv9RKY9N7f9tkBXDr91iKZd\nCGaQZULop81GbueIDhMMpPBVfoLLL1QMD2znqnW3bkrojk7wnqJuGyMkQrQwgNdG\nkMvCUflfpotDWuZT4LcM\n=RCz8\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-18T21:41:04Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web