OWJhMGMxZDAzNTIxNjM5NjA2NmI3MWE1NTg3ZjRlZTgzMjNhOTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/6de961c03a3ca53d108c42b67f84e10595fa601a","sha":"6de961c03a3ca53d108c42b67f84e10595fa601a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/6de961c03a3ca53d108c42b67f84e10595fa601a"}],"sha":"ad9ba0c1d035216396066b71a5587f4ee8323a92","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/ad9ba0c1d035216396066b71a5587f4ee8323a92"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/6de961c03a3ca53d108c42b67f84e10595fa601a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-10T21:29:38Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-10T21:29:38Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"refactor(container): poll for the manager instead of reading it once (#134)\n\nThe manager global was read ONCE, synchronously, at the top of the managed\nstart path. That worked only because Signal K starts plugins alphabetically\nand \"signalk-questdb\" sorts after \"signalk-container\" -- a rename, or a\nsignalk-container slow enough to still be pulling its own image, and this\nplugin reported \"signalk-container plugin required\" on a system that has it.\n\nwaitForContainerManager does both phases: poll for the global, then wait for\nruntime detection to settle. It returns manager and runtime separately, so\nthe two failure modes keep their distinct messages rather than collapsing\ninto one.\n\nThe abort signal is threaded through, so a purge preempting a start stops\nthe poll immediately instead of waiting out the 120s budget. The existing\npost-await abort check stays -- the helper''s wait returns rather than\nthrowing, and the caller decides whether an abort is an error.\n\nDeliberately NOT adopting ManagedContainer.start() here. It bundles\nmanager-wait, ensureRunning and HTTP readiness into one call, but this\nplugin interleaves its own work between those steps: abort checks, endpoint\nresolution, and a readiness probe that runs SELECT 1 through QueryClient\nrather than pinging a path. Replacing the sequence would mean giving up the\nreal query probe or faking it through a custom fetchImpl.\n\nVerified against the live container, not just unit tests: cold start\nresolved both phases and reached ILP connected, restore replayed 98\ncontexts / 412 values, /api/status reported running with 38.4M rows and no\nunstorable paths, a plugin disable stopped the container cleanly (143), and\na re-enable brought it back through the same path. Zero questdb error lines\nacross the run.\n\nRefs #131\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"2f2f60cf957098c7866541b3237350aae4320676","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/2f2f60cf957098c7866541b3237350aae4320676"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/6de961c03a3ca53d108c42b67f84e10595fa601a","verification":{"payload":"tree 2f2f60cf957098c7866541b3237350aae4320676\nparent b0c60a99617f00384f89629697b71a3953e50b69\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1786397378 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786397378 +1200\n\nrefactor(container): poll for the manager instead of reading it once (#134)\n\nThe manager global was read ONCE, synchronously, at the top of the managed\nstart path. That worked only because Signal K starts plugins alphabetically\nand \"signalk-questdb\" sorts after \"signalk-container\" -- a rename, or a\nsignalk-container slow enough to still be pulling its own image, and this\nplugin reported \"signalk-container plugin required\" on a system that has it.\n\nwaitForContainerManager does both phases: poll for the global, then wait for\nruntime detection to settle. It returns manager and runtime separately, so\nthe two failure modes keep their distinct messages rather than collapsing\ninto one.\n\nThe abort signal is threaded through, so a purge preempting a start stops\nthe poll immediately instead of waiting out the 120s budget. The existing\npost-await abort check stays -- the helper''s wait returns rather than\nthrowing, and the caller decides whether an abort is an error.\n\nDeliberately NOT adopting ManagedContainer.start() here. It bundles\nmanager-wait, ensureRunning and HTTP readiness into one call, but this\nplugin interleaves its own work between those steps: abort checks, endpoint\nresolution, and a readiness probe that runs SELECT 1 through QueryClient\nrather than pinging a path. Replacing the sequence would mean giving up the\nreal query probe or faking it through a custom fetchImpl.\n\nVerified against the live container, not just unit tests: cold start\nresolved both phases and reached ILP connected, restore replayed 98\ncontexts / 412 values, /api/status reported running with 38.4M rows and no\nunstorable paths, a plugin disable stopped the container cleanly (143), and\na re-enable brought it back through the same path. Zero questdb error lines\nacross the run.\n\nRefs #131\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqekLCCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAdI0QACOmjZPR67kgg2u/tD/ztP4f\nHsOLPZEtLFQLCHVyzwsRlBjrMQ82UmyQvNtXcaJbHywlIAo9ND46T7n4gtFo0yUo\nVCyyNPY0xY1GWzO+dEduRmTM0weO/FGt3PPRt7vbXFL5VnTKdyz4mUn9v/hS2Ti5\njiI3Um0EEjSpLUu705qyocxRq/dgDHsHqIsAfujHuMit9fFrR5/DUN46kKR4rFCJ\n5F5mf8Jh2R5BlC0gSXPAitxPKfeZH0cEkIDeCCVX6aWEHt+89gfde5pS2j8K7sSj\nPGuC8zHSLYj3CSYVOfp55cigpmnNHRn9/IzfeTy02E5rlAVFDdmztjsdQ9gL05QH\ntovJ1maU3Y52HRzxhgxie7sps6CMBnV2NhwUZVMYS1LNO9S1SrY4P6HTGmofhW7E\n7k4uIIfz4L+xWRUZAINllXTBz+Yn1zp1szkC/G6cmxAlJTBNWunL1qitObLr6Onk\nFjqXKetD02J1qTPjOxxEr58ztwAHoNrJpXGQaKO4uSjUmV8R6zM7pmp6k6Dj7TVm\nH2udCTz2UGFNRf4N/5iO51nC7DFo9vJbyYqYosbcpBCK7LRVeq2unhqzu/UWADvf\ntKCeEseXdb5n4SKDG5eIAcsQvviYdgOrAQ+3/No2OJOc24abo2Fdfsaz6TxLl5xC\n6+PfcC0HJ1NoKyeJHjm0\n=Nm2z\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-10T21:29:47Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/6de961c03a3ca53d108c42b67f84e10595fa601a","node_id":"C_kwDOR56yYtoAKDZkZTk2MWMwM2EzY2E1M2QxMDhjNDJiNjdmODRlMTA1OTVmYTYwMWE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/b0c60a99617f00384f89629697b71a3953e50b69","sha":"b0c60a99617f00384f89629697b71a3953e50b69","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/b0c60a99617f00384f89629697b71a3953e50b69"}],"sha":"6de961c03a3ca53d108c42b67f84e10595fa601a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/6de961c03a3ca53d108c42b67f84e10595fa601a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/b0c60a99617f00384f89629697b71a3953e50b69/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-10T21:15:33Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-10T21:15:33Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"refactor(types): import signalk-container''s types instead of mirroring them (#133)\n\n* refactor(types): import signalk-container''s types instead of mirroring them\n\nDeletes the 156-line hand-written copy of signalk-container''s API. It\ndrifted by construction: it was only ever updated when this plugin happened\nto need a new member, so getContainerNofile sat unmirrored from 1.25.3 until\na probe needed it. signalk-container-helper carries the same types and pins\nthem against signalk-container/types in CI, so drift fails there rather than\nhere.\n\nThe swap immediately surfaced two real defects the mirror had hidden:\n\n- ensureNetwork was called unguarded at one of its two call sites. The\n  helper types every version-gated member as optional so a plugin degrades\n  on an older signalk-container; the local mirror declared it required. The\n  other call site was already feature-detecting connectToNetwork, so the two\n  disagreed about the same API.\n\n- The helper''s own SelfDeploymentResult was wrong -- it declared status and\n  remediation behind an index signature, typing isContainerized as unknown.\n  questdb''s local declaration was correct and disagreed with it. Fixed\n  upstream in signalk-container-helper 0.4.3 rather than worked around here.\n\nUlimitClampStatus in api-contract.ts becomes a re-export of the helper''s\nUlimitClamp. `import type` is load-bearing there: that file is shared with\nthe browser panel, and the helper''s main entry carries ~19KB of Node-side\nruntime. Verified the federated bundle stays clean.\n\nDeploymentResolver in questdb-endpoint.ts deliberately stays a narrow\nstructural type rather than becoming ContainerManagerApi -- a real manager\nsatisfies it, while a test fake needs one method returning one field instead\nof all twelve fields of SelfDeploymentResult.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* fix(container): feature-detect ensureNetwork without dropping the network\n\nMy guard was wrong in both directions, and would have broken Grafana\nconnectivity on an older signalk-container.\n\nRequiring BOTH ensureNetwork and connectToNetwork skipped attachment\nentirely when only the latter exists. And gating the networkMode assignment\non ensureNetwork dropped the container off the network for the same reason.\n\nCreating the network is the optional step; joining it is what the companion\nGrafana depends on. On a signalk-container old enough to lack ensureNetwork,\nthe network the operator named almost certainly exists already -- so attach\nregardless, and only skip the create.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit. Its other finding asked to restore the 156-line local\ntype mirror, which is the thing this PR removes; skipped.\n\nRefs #131\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"3ce4254e58c4c46bdd463227767aa6586fb57f7f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/3ce4254e58c4c46bdd463227767aa6586fb57f7f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/b0c60a99617f00384f89629697b71a3953e50b69","verification":{"payload":"tree 3ce4254e58c4c46bdd463227767aa6586fb57f7f\nparent 9613698a443f3a5a166762ed92ecc256a3f5b662\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1786396533 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786396533 +1200\n\nrefactor(types): import signalk-container''s types instead of mirroring them (#133)\n\n* refactor(types): import signalk-container''s types instead of mirroring them\n\nDeletes the 156-line hand-written copy of signalk-container''s API. It\ndrifted by construction: it was only ever updated when this plugin happened\nto need a new member, so getContainerNofile sat unmirrored from 1.25.3 until\na probe needed it. signalk-container-helper carries the same types and pins\nthem against signalk-container/types in CI, so drift fails there rather than\nhere.\n\nThe swap immediately surfaced two real defects the mirror had hidden:\n\n- ensureNetwork was called unguarded at one of its two call sites. The\n  helper types every version-gated member as optional so a plugin degrades\n  on an older signalk-container; the local mirror declared it required. The\n  other call site was already feature-detecting connectToNetwork, so the two\n  disagreed about the same API.\n\n- The helper''s own SelfDeploymentResult was wrong -- it declared status and\n  remediation behind an index signature, typing isContainerized as unknown.\n  questdb''s local declaration was correct and disagreed with it. Fixed\n  upstream in signalk-container-helper 0.4.3 rather than worked around here.\n\nUlimitClampStatus in api-contract.ts becomes a re-export of the helper''s\nUlimitClamp. `import type` is load-bearing there: that file is shared with\nthe browser panel, and the helper''s main entry carries ~19KB of Node-side\nruntime. Verified the federated bundle stays clean.\n\nDeploymentResolver in questdb-endpoint.ts deliberately stays a narrow\nstructural type rather than becoming ContainerManagerApi -- a real manager\nsatisfies it, while a test fake needs one method returning one field instead\nof all twelve fields of SelfDeploymentResult.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* fix(container): feature-detect ensureNetwork without dropping the network\n\nMy guard was wrong in both directions, and would have broken Grafana\nconnectivity on an older signalk-container.\n\nRequiring BOTH ensureNetwork and connectToNetwork skipped attachment\nentirely when only the latter exists. And gating the networkMode assignment\non ensureNetwork dropped the container off the network for the same reason.\n\nCreating the network is the optional step; joining it is what the companion\nGrafana depends on. On a signalk-container old enough to lack ensureNetwork,\nthe network the operator named almost certainly exists already -- so attach\nregardless, and only skip the create.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit. Its other finding asked to restore the 156-line local\ntype mirror, which is the thing this PR removes; skipped.\n\nRefs #131\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqej91CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAATREQAKvGUP9Kon1mIEXK2z7WKoai\n6juT6gtGy77qbB4RL1viVJugYBUw/scIhRpXDI8WiT3TyqOIwgQz3javZNvcNk1L\nBWXO43f3qwXpy0hFWwTmuticPR4j4l+JH1Nns2tMQtWUjIJHL9u4namxqEz4Rsya\neYsg4WA4VkZDHnINYcRb91he7ovEyv3WHYBub2lfSSoUO3WgnOsd8mJP/XKvAzyP\np3aNTot3FrGVRVBA8qTmDRkYeExXSLRyf+w1zfA9fIEpUVDNneB5KUxYn1PeAw4P\nKp299RrGAHbGDpv5O7xxnUe7fFFu0dMYUr2k9PjZowqKcUn313XObnMQR504hZ4Y\nl5CvJSjNFoBsF+Nblw6+AN388iyzEad6YjYCwpnZIwDBgtqknjNri6NTYZTxw73W\nOvdtgl3/ab523TvzmlFHRi/asn4z8cV45mTFqu/i9l7nFGxNdqSiJFlJlXQAZeXa\nVZcgNaONamwUm9HpywI4JhJg3TlQI1s+jltn+smRmN4MgNhn/ysvLWv4g3maxpPC\nYkKkqGi7vQDutXcITPjnGCIt496pXdzbcGpVxqAES0Rzt0DQ8onZkAFSHpdqM7Y8\nS2u8jv31TgYa6eMgJbN1yHg3hmG3mM144y3d841hRGCPPcKD8HUF7YypqFSw4sPc\npUGjuXso2nyPAz+trb5U\n=EZzs\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-10T21:16:53Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/b0c60a99617f00384f89629697b71a3953e50b69","node_id":"C_kwDOR56yYtoAKGIwYzYwYTk5NjE3ZjAwMzg0Zjg5NjI5Njk3YjcxYTM5NTNlNTBiNjk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/9613698a443f3a5a166762ed92ecc256a3f5b662","sha":"9613698a443f3a5a166762ed92ecc256a3f5b662","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/9613698a443f3a5a166762ed92ecc256a3f5b662"}],"sha":"b0c60a99617f00384f89629697b71a3953e50b69","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/b0c60a99617f00384f89629697b71a3953e50b69"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/9613698a443f3a5a166762ed92ecc256a3f5b662/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-10T19:25:51Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-10T19:25:51Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"refactor(panel): adopt signalk-container-helper UI building blocks (#132)\n\n* refactor(panel): use signalk-container-helper VersionSelect\n\nThe hand-rolled version <select> was controlled on questdbVersion but\nrendered no option for a value outside the listed releases. A tag pinned\nbeyond the top 3, or ANY tag when GitHub rate-limited the listing and the\narray came back empty, left the box showing \"latest\" while state still held\nthe real tag -- and doSave writes questdbVersion unconditionally, so the\nnext Save silently changed the running image.\n\nVersionSelect injects a synthetic \"<tag> (running)\" option for exactly that\ncase. Verified against the helper''s own runningTagFallback: a pin aged out\nof the top-3 and a rate-limited empty list both now resolve to the pinned\ntag instead of falling through to latest.\n\nThe helper is zero-dependency with react as a peer, and its /ui entry\nimports no Node builtins, so the federated browser bundle stays clean --\nconfirmed by the typebox/fs grep over public/.\n\nFirst slice of the container-helper adoption; lifecycle follows separately.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): use helper StatusCard and useVersions\n\nStatusCard replaces both status cards. The not-running one drops its\nexplicit #fef2f2/#ef4444 pair: the error state supplies that same muted-red\ntreatment, which is where the local colours came from.\n\nuseVersions replaces fetchVersions, and fixes a second way the version\ndropdown could empty itself. The local version called setVersions on every\nres.ok, so a 200 carrying non-JSON — a captive portal, an auth redirect, a\nproxy error page — normalised to [] and wiped the list. The helper keeps the\nlast good list when a fetch fails, and reports the failure as an operator\nmessage instead. An empty dropdown is what lets a Save silently change the\nrunning image, so this closes the same class of bug as VersionSelect.\n\nIt also fetches on mount itself, so the local effect drops that call and\npolls status only.\n\ntoVersionList is now dead — superseded by the helper''s deriveVersionsView,\nwhich additionally guards non-array members and reports source-level\nrate-limiting. Removed with its tests; the shared not-JSON test keeps its\nmigration-source coverage.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): layer styles on helper panelStyles\n\nstyles.ts drops from 237 lines to 79. The helper already carried 32 of the\n39 keys with byte-identical values -- they were hand-copied between the\ncontainer plugins before the library existed -- so only what is genuinely\nquestdb''s own is declared now, and a change to the shared look reaches every\npanel instead of drifting per plugin.\n\nbtnDanger takes the HELPER''s version rather than this panel''s larger\npadding/fontSize. That is a deliberate visual change: converging on the\nshared sizing is the point of adopting panelStyles, and both call sites --\nskipping a WAL segment and purging all data -- read fine at the standard\nsize, arguably better for destructive actions.\n\nVerified against the built output that all 39 previous keys still resolve,\nthat the 7 questdb-only ones survive the spread, and that btnDanger is the\nonly value that changed.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): use helper Button and ActionStatus\n\nEight hand-styled buttons become <Button>, and the outcome line becomes\n<ActionStatus>. Each button was the same shape: spread S.btn, spread a\nvariant, conditionally spread S.btnDisabled, then a ternary swapping the\nlabel for a busy string. busy/busyLabel expresses that directly, and the\ncomponent derives disabled from busy so the two cannot disagree -- three of\nthese listed a busy flag in `disabled` as well, which was duplication\nwaiting to drift.\n\nTwo keep an explicit style: the update row''s buttons run smaller than a form\nbutton because they sit inline with status text, and the helper''s `small`\n(4px 10px / 11px) is a shade tighter than what that row was built around.\nUPDATE_BTN names the intent rather than repeating the literals.\n\nSave keeps btnSave -- questdb''s own blue, distinct from the helper''s\nprimary, marking the one action that commits the form.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): use helper useStatusPoll\n\nReplaces fetchStatus plus its setInterval. The hook self-schedules each\npoll after the previous one settles, so a response slower than the 5s period\ncannot stack overlapping requests, and a generation counter drops stale\nresponses that land after newer ones. The panel already did exactly this for\nWAL diagnosis; status was the one poll still on setInterval.\n\nBehaviour is otherwise preserved: the body is parsed on non-2xx too, because\nthe unhealthy 503 carries fields the panel must still surface\n(hostMaxMapCount, since mmap exhaustion is one of the ways QuestDB becomes\nunhealthy), and an unparseable body falls back to the not_running shape.\n\nThe five post-action re-polls become the hook''s refresh().\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): use helper CollapsibleSection and formatNumber\n\nBoth local definitions were byte-equivalent to the helper''s once 0.4.1\ncarried the #123 affordance fix upstream. Adopting them before that release\nwould have silently reintroduced the bug -- the helper''s header was still\nsectionTitle grey.\n\nsectionToggle and sectionMarker go with them: they existed only to style the\nlocal component, and keeping a second copy alongside the helper''s is exactly\nhow the two drift apart. styles.ts is now 60 lines, from 237.\n\nThe panel regression suite still passes against the helper''s component --\naria-expanded, auto-open when patterns are configured, and every section\ncollapsed except a configured Path filtering -- and the shipped bundle\ncarries #555 with an 11px marker from a single definition.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): drop redundant btnSave, keep the defaultOpen caveat\n\nTwo things found auditing the branch.\n\nbtnSave was byte-identical to the helper''s btnPrimary -- which is Button''s\ndefault variant -- so `style={S.btnSave}` was a no-op dressed as intent. My\nearlier commit message claimed it was \"questdb''s own blue, distinct from the\nhelper''s primary\"; that was simply wrong. Removed both the key and the prop.\n\nDeleting the local CollapsibleSection also deleted the one place that\nrecorded WHY defaultOpen is safe: it is read once on mount, which works only\nbecause signalk-server hands the panel its configuration before first render\n(useState(plugin.data.configuration), passed straight down). A caller\nderiving it from data the panel fetches itself would seed false forever and\nlose the behaviour with no error. The helper does not document that, so the\ncaveat moves to the call site that depends on it.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): drop two echo comments\n\nThe {/* Status */} and {/* Save */} markers labelled anonymous divs that no\nlonger exist -- <ActionStatus> and a Button reading \"Save Configuration\"\nname themselves. Raised by CodeRabbit.\n\nRefs #131\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"1f09fe10e1af22bdb132f74e8a4a0eb8717ca3fe","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/1f09fe10e1af22bdb132f74e8a4a0eb8717ca3fe"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/9613698a443f3a5a166762ed92ecc256a3f5b662","verification":{"payload":"tree 1f09fe10e1af22bdb132f74e8a4a0eb8717ca3fe\nparent 14e85ae2536f159c5217f560e97e125b956b5fa8\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1786389951 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786389951 +1200\n\nrefactor(panel): adopt signalk-container-helper UI building blocks (#132)\n\n* refactor(panel): use signalk-container-helper VersionSelect\n\nThe hand-rolled version <select> was controlled on questdbVersion but\nrendered no option for a value outside the listed releases. A tag pinned\nbeyond the top 3, or ANY tag when GitHub rate-limited the listing and the\narray came back empty, left the box showing \"latest\" while state still held\nthe real tag -- and doSave writes questdbVersion unconditionally, so the\nnext Save silently changed the running image.\n\nVersionSelect injects a synthetic \"<tag> (running)\" option for exactly that\ncase. Verified against the helper''s own runningTagFallback: a pin aged out\nof the top-3 and a rate-limited empty list both now resolve to the pinned\ntag instead of falling through to latest.\n\nThe helper is zero-dependency with react as a peer, and its /ui entry\nimports no Node builtins, so the federated browser bundle stays clean --\nconfirmed by the typebox/fs grep over public/.\n\nFirst slice of the container-helper adoption; lifecycle follows separately.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): use helper StatusCard and useVersions\n\nStatusCard replaces both status cards. The not-running one drops its\nexplicit #fef2f2/#ef4444 pair: the error state supplies that same muted-red\ntreatment, which is where the local colours came from.\n\nuseVersions replaces fetchVersions, and fixes a second way the version\ndropdown could empty itself. The local version called setVersions on every\nres.ok, so a 200 carrying non-JSON — a captive portal, an auth redirect, a\nproxy error page — normalised to [] and wiped the list. The helper keeps the\nlast good list when a fetch fails, and reports the failure as an operator\nmessage instead. An empty dropdown is what lets a Save silently change the\nrunning image, so this closes the same class of bug as VersionSelect.\n\nIt also fetches on mount itself, so the local effect drops that call and\npolls status only.\n\ntoVersionList is now dead — superseded by the helper''s deriveVersionsView,\nwhich additionally guards non-array members and reports source-level\nrate-limiting. Removed with its tests; the shared not-JSON test keeps its\nmigration-source coverage.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): layer styles on helper panelStyles\n\nstyles.ts drops from 237 lines to 79. The helper already carried 32 of the\n39 keys with byte-identical values -- they were hand-copied between the\ncontainer plugins before the library existed -- so only what is genuinely\nquestdb''s own is declared now, and a change to the shared look reaches every\npanel instead of drifting per plugin.\n\nbtnDanger takes the HELPER''s version rather than this panel''s larger\npadding/fontSize. That is a deliberate visual change: converging on the\nshared sizing is the point of adopting panelStyles, and both call sites --\nskipping a WAL segment and purging all data -- read fine at the standard\nsize, arguably better for destructive actions.\n\nVerified against the built output that all 39 previous keys still resolve,\nthat the 7 questdb-only ones survive the spread, and that btnDanger is the\nonly value that changed.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): use helper Button and ActionStatus\n\nEight hand-styled buttons become <Button>, and the outcome line becomes\n<ActionStatus>. Each button was the same shape: spread S.btn, spread a\nvariant, conditionally spread S.btnDisabled, then a ternary swapping the\nlabel for a busy string. busy/busyLabel expresses that directly, and the\ncomponent derives disabled from busy so the two cannot disagree -- three of\nthese listed a busy flag in `disabled` as well, which was duplication\nwaiting to drift.\n\nTwo keep an explicit style: the update row''s buttons run smaller than a form\nbutton because they sit inline with status text, and the helper''s `small`\n(4px 10px / 11px) is a shade tighter than what that row was built around.\nUPDATE_BTN names the intent rather than repeating the literals.\n\nSave keeps btnSave -- questdb''s own blue, distinct from the helper''s\nprimary, marking the one action that commits the form.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): use helper useStatusPoll\n\nReplaces fetchStatus plus its setInterval. The hook self-schedules each\npoll after the previous one settles, so a response slower than the 5s period\ncannot stack overlapping requests, and a generation counter drops stale\nresponses that land after newer ones. The panel already did exactly this for\nWAL diagnosis; status was the one poll still on setInterval.\n\nBehaviour is otherwise preserved: the body is parsed on non-2xx too, because\nthe unhealthy 503 carries fields the panel must still surface\n(hostMaxMapCount, since mmap exhaustion is one of the ways QuestDB becomes\nunhealthy), and an unparseable body falls back to the not_running shape.\n\nThe five post-action re-polls become the hook''s refresh().\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): use helper CollapsibleSection and formatNumber\n\nBoth local definitions were byte-equivalent to the helper''s once 0.4.1\ncarried the #123 affordance fix upstream. Adopting them before that release\nwould have silently reintroduced the bug -- the helper''s header was still\nsectionTitle grey.\n\nsectionToggle and sectionMarker go with them: they existed only to style the\nlocal component, and keeping a second copy alongside the helper''s is exactly\nhow the two drift apart. styles.ts is now 60 lines, from 237.\n\nThe panel regression suite still passes against the helper''s component --\naria-expanded, auto-open when patterns are configured, and every section\ncollapsed except a configured Path filtering -- and the shipped bundle\ncarries #555 with an 11px marker from a single definition.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): drop redundant btnSave, keep the defaultOpen caveat\n\nTwo things found auditing the branch.\n\nbtnSave was byte-identical to the helper''s btnPrimary -- which is Button''s\ndefault variant -- so `style={S.btnSave}` was a no-op dressed as intent. My\nearlier commit message claimed it was \"questdb''s own blue, distinct from the\nhelper''s primary\"; that was simply wrong. Removed both the key and the prop.\n\nDeleting the local CollapsibleSection also deleted the one place that\nrecorded WHY defaultOpen is safe: it is read once on mount, which works only\nbecause signalk-server hands the panel its configuration before first render\n(useState(plugin.data.configuration), passed straight down). A caller\nderiving it from data the panel fetches itself would seed false forever and\nlose the behaviour with no error. The helper does not document that, so the\ncaveat moves to the call site that depends on it.\n\nRefs #131\n\n* refactor(panel): drop two echo comments\n\nThe {/* Status */} and {/* Save */} markers labelled anonymous divs that no\nlonger exist -- <ActionStatus> and a Button reading \"Save Configuration\"\nname themselves. Raised by CodeRabbit.\n\nRefs #131\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqeiW/CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4b4QAAOLHbmRRwsQiP2kBvDO6TQU\n/VPe/FiwCBHgLdI3g0oTjoMSC1CcRbWx8/MLjy7sMLA224n5IibKETDc25Tkeox+\nTVpMezEzteqsOGf7hEabQXz4MuJgVpcrngLNvj+gwaVfWA1EQSqaMMJPKPUrfrBM\neHIMyeMp0lppOp8ovnaLIokleLDEE14wIs91v34Bw6H3Waan0uyvCaxDlsjiRyLd\nNjd2oyBUo1pvPuM8SkwXYwuRArVY4LL3f8gABxh+Mh7EvGBSHykOn6npBTu4dKeO\no0RUKN/OcBTKtC4Jem1GDbIft1h8Yfick2hbuMOpvO/aJWCkwu6WLepixXogVRIl\nFsuNGYzxDYbkl48/64/USBzmN6VFqpB0/FEnrxZxxIobMM4umwjBr+eQL4fTr2Dj\n+GpgVRmsuEMc3xjNurU5FM/4tOxSy3f9HH6M2AOy4TY2eHTpdjg7/ERWEB0NM/fF\nNBafx6G701bguD9cgzwzPK1GibUg8XTtfmySYl/i2uVOOpABNFR0D/CQEOuR2J7V\nDerkATGaYMItKnZCQRoWmaqHKhyJ6P2lMyINJ+E1P491xiomgJVrZriziVw8JAWA\nhE8JvUu0dR7lohppfkaEuy/vjpdUrD651bUscQ84WEptoKYDasrrdNvL2OBw/d2H\n2WDTz+ObMyEKd63rnvHv\n=1f9r\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-10T19:26:07Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/9613698a443f3a5a166762ed92ecc256a3f5b662","node_id":"C_kwDOR56yYtoAKDk2MTM2OThhNDQzZjNhNWExNjY3NjJlZDkyZWNjMjU2YTNmNWI2NjI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/14e85ae2536f159c5217f560e97e125b956b5fa8","sha":"14e85ae2536f159c5217f560e97e125b956b5fa8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/14e85ae2536f159c5217f560e97e125b956b5fa8"}],"sha":"9613698a443f3a5a166762ed92ecc256a3f5b662","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/9613698a443f3a5a166762ed92ecc256a3f5b662"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/14e85ae2536f159c5217f560e97e125b956b5fa8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-10T17:53:49Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-10T17:53:49Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(restore): look identity up over a fixed 24h window (#130)\n\n* fix(restore): look identity up over a fixed 24h window\n\nRestored AIS targets came back unnamed. The restore window is tuned to\nposition, which arrives every few seconds, but the AIS static report\ncarrying a vessel''s name repeats only every ~6 minutes and only while it is\nin range. One window for both meant a target whose name landed 12 minutes\nago restored as an unnamed contact.\n\nMeasured on a live install at the DEFAULT 9-minute window: 75 vessels\nrestored with a position, 0 of them carrying a name. After: 77 restored, 74\nnamed.\n\nSplit the allowlist into MOTION_PATHS and IDENTITY_PATHS and give identity\na flat 24 hours. Not a multiple of maxAgeMs -- identity has nothing to do\nwith how fresh a fix must be, and scaling off it left the default at 6h,\nwhich reached exactly ONE named vessel on a quiet day and so still restored\nnothing at the setting users actually run.\n\nThere is no staleness risk to trade against. An MMSI is a unique, durable\nidentifier -- across a week of real AIS traffic here not one vessel\nbroadcast a different name -- so an older name is not a wronger name. The\ncost is flat too: LATEST ON collapses to one row per (path, context), so\nwidening changes which row is newest, not how many return.\n\nThe row-level age check had to move too. It re-applied maxAgeMs to every row\nin JS, so identity fetched by the widened SQL was immediately discarded as\nstale -- the fix was inert until both agreed.\n\nThe position gate is untouched: a vessel with only an old name still\nrestores nothing, so reaching further back cannot resurrect ghost targets.\n\nVerified against a live database, and by regression tests that fail on the\npre-fix code (2 of 33) and pass after.\n\nCloses #127\n\n* fix(recording): refuse non-finite top-level numbers\n\nA NaN or +/-Infinity arriving as a plain delta value routed to \"number\" and\nwas written verbatim into the ILP line. QuestDB does NOT reject it --\nverified against a live instance, `value=NaN` is accepted and stored -- so\none faulty sensor reading poisons the numeric column and every aggregate\nover that path afterwards.\n\nflattenObjectValue already guarded its leaves against exactly this. The\ntop-level path did not, which is the inconsistency: the same NaN was dropped\ninside an object and stored outside one.\n\nFound in review of #128.\n\nRefs #128\n\n* fix(restore): drop the dead mmsi path, guard empty-path flattening\n\nTwo findings from auditing why no mmsi row has ever been recorded.\n\nIt is not a recording bug. For another vessel the MMSI IS the context --\nvessels.urn:mrn:imo:mmsi:244813000, built by the decoder from the AIS userId\n-- and neither n2k-signalk nor nmea0183-signalk emits it as a path. For self\nit is server configuration written once via setSelfValue, not a delta, and\nsignalk-server''s staticDataFilter strips incoming attempts to set it. So\nevery MMSI is already stored, in the context column of every row. The\nallowlist entry only widened the SQL IN list for a path that cannot exist;\nthis database has never held one.\n\nSeparately, flattenObjectValue would build \".name\" for an empty parent path\n-- a leading-dot path matching no Signal K path and no filter pattern. It\ncannot fire today because the recorder returns before routing when path is\n\"\" (that shape is the AIS static report, handled by extractVesselName), and\nthe live database confirms zero leading-dot rows. But the function should\nnot depend on a caller''s guard to avoid emitting a malformed path, so it now\nreturns empty itself.\n\nRestore verified unchanged against a live database after the removal: 79\ncontexts, 76 named.\n\nRefs #127\n\n* fix(restore): keep names identifiable through the value_kind fallback\n\nWhen the value_kind column is missing, the fallback query reported NULL for\nevery row''s kind. That failed the `kind === \"identity\"` gate, so a vessel\nname replayed as a literal `name` path instead of the empty-path object\nFreeboard reads -- leaving targets unnamed on an unmigrated table for the\nsame visible reason as #127.\n\nSynthesize \"identity\" for the synthetic name path instead. Sound because\nthat path is only ever written by the identity writer, and on a table\nwithout value_kind there is no way to distinguish it from a data path\nliterally called \"name\" anyway.\n\nPre-existing, not introduced here, but it produces exactly the symptom this\nPR fixes. Raised by CodeRabbit; the existing fallback test asserted the\nretry happened but returned an empty dataset, so it never exercised name\nclassification.\n\nRefs #127\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"ccd74b78568666a6356b4d62790ddf130aad2869","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/ccd74b78568666a6356b4d62790ddf130aad2869"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/14e85ae2536f159c5217f560e97e125b956b5fa8","verification":{"payload":"tree ccd74b78568666a6356b4d62790ddf130aad2869\nparent 04456543e3ee5651f4af0ddf8107774beccd750e\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1786384429 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786384429 +1200\n\nfix(restore): look identity up over a fixed 24h window (#130)\n\n* fix(restore): look identity up over a fixed 24h window\n\nRestored AIS targets came back unnamed. The restore window is tuned to\nposition, which arrives every few seconds, but the AIS static report\ncarrying a vessel''s name repeats only every ~6 minutes and only while it is\nin range. One window for both meant a target whose name landed 12 minutes\nago restored as an unnamed contact.\n\nMeasured on a live install at the DEFAULT 9-minute window: 75 vessels\nrestored with a position, 0 of them carrying a name. After: 77 restored, 74\nnamed.\n\nSplit the allowlist into MOTION_PATHS and IDENTITY_PATHS and give identity\na flat 24 hours. Not a multiple of maxAgeMs -- identity has nothing to do\nwith how fresh a fix must be, and scaling off it left the default at 6h,\nwhich reached exactly ONE named vessel on a quiet day and so still restored\nnothing at the setting users actually run.\n\nThere is no staleness risk to trade against. An MMSI is a unique, durable\nidentifier -- across a week of real AIS traffic here not one vessel\nbroadcast a different name -- so an older name is not a wronger name. The\ncost is flat too: LATEST ON collapses to one row per (path, context), so\nwidening changes which row is newest, not how many return.\n\nThe row-level age check had to move too. It re-applied maxAgeMs to every row\nin JS, so identity fetched by the widened SQL was immediately discarded as\nstale -- the fix was inert until both agreed.\n\nThe position gate is untouched: a vessel with only an old name still\nrestores nothing, so reaching further back cannot resurrect ghost targets.\n\nVerified against a live database, and by regression tests that fail on the\npre-fix code (2 of 33) and pass after.\n\nCloses #127\n\n* fix(recording): refuse non-finite top-level numbers\n\nA NaN or +/-Infinity arriving as a plain delta value routed to \"number\" and\nwas written verbatim into the ILP line. QuestDB does NOT reject it --\nverified against a live instance, `value=NaN` is accepted and stored -- so\none faulty sensor reading poisons the numeric column and every aggregate\nover that path afterwards.\n\nflattenObjectValue already guarded its leaves against exactly this. The\ntop-level path did not, which is the inconsistency: the same NaN was dropped\ninside an object and stored outside one.\n\nFound in review of #128.\n\nRefs #128\n\n* fix(restore): drop the dead mmsi path, guard empty-path flattening\n\nTwo findings from auditing why no mmsi row has ever been recorded.\n\nIt is not a recording bug. For another vessel the MMSI IS the context --\nvessels.urn:mrn:imo:mmsi:244813000, built by the decoder from the AIS userId\n-- and neither n2k-signalk nor nmea0183-signalk emits it as a path. For self\nit is server configuration written once via setSelfValue, not a delta, and\nsignalk-server''s staticDataFilter strips incoming attempts to set it. So\nevery MMSI is already stored, in the context column of every row. The\nallowlist entry only widened the SQL IN list for a path that cannot exist;\nthis database has never held one.\n\nSeparately, flattenObjectValue would build \".name\" for an empty parent path\n-- a leading-dot path matching no Signal K path and no filter pattern. It\ncannot fire today because the recorder returns before routing when path is\n\"\" (that shape is the AIS static report, handled by extractVesselName), and\nthe live database confirms zero leading-dot rows. But the function should\nnot depend on a caller''s guard to avoid emitting a malformed path, so it now\nreturns empty itself.\n\nRestore verified unchanged against a live database after the removal: 79\ncontexts, 76 named.\n\nRefs #127\n\n* fix(restore): keep names identifiable through the value_kind fallback\n\nWhen the value_kind column is missing, the fallback query reported NULL for\nevery row''s kind. That failed the `kind === \"identity\"` gate, so a vessel\nname replayed as a literal `name` path instead of the empty-path object\nFreeboard reads -- leaving targets unnamed on an unmigrated table for the\nsame visible reason as #127.\n\nSynthesize \"identity\" for the synthetic name path instead. Sound because\nthat path is only ever written by the identity writer, and on a table\nwithout value_kind there is no way to distinguish it from a data path\nliterally called \"name\" anyway.\n\nPre-existing, not introduced here, but it produces exactly the symptom this\nPR fixes. Raised by CodeRabbit; the existing fallback test asserted the\nretry happened but returned an empty dataset, so it never exercised name\nclassification.\n\nRefs #127\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqehAtCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAZYsQAHDN8ky2WBr7x8+PE23M1TNw\nJ8mTWYtcZIW9FkEb4N9y59bXLJPR/0ofO8Gl1s2AZJKqGCWfu7CxYSg2b7IMTSHH\nTd3dJucXuH0/ImQ4eN1ldzNYgWe9dDnUOHnVLokLn2VjmAWZkp2x/eRS4sD8OMCP\nKkbdNOpF6c6hUHvYsaGmpHU97i471GVo75hm87HV0Upb+z1ZaCWk/dfYVyBa90DC\nLB0WeQcMBA9ni40lO7m7TeClNyePClWy7EbcM5x6o+/sCMlKeswIQNBtWANn39uE\n1fNaCvKd13xLvrie2FSsSaH4Oac47h4zA52cSW/mlg4vfurzOUB77qQ+z1p2hj2n\nLdcT+l1SlNcAG0CJVBb+8K0hyA7zXWv4asI7uQXuql86hmWN2j290JeNX5VDb11c\nG3smEOUljKAGStwzE9tQhdDWMnypGI34sV63wNlYxci4CZPx9r+RukSTl3ho77GG\n8sOpVLss0P7D+r7qGrxLD6VFU6veUEmdnpDkfc6QwUSsfK0uXcl/Qqll/Q3Li+K8\njhXTzHUg6i4IzrxVhJ00gQdoCFL6SPB2IUvwAIAcusk31GPBWmw9v7YzUd/qHeqq\nWo7f6FbLJUaTw+Oc+PzOFxfk9lzWVTCrliGQonf+NsFbK44bleYBfMHDg5NNAVol\nOBiwzCF1PFOFi70ASSPC\n=FHRA\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-10T19:10:53Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/14e85ae2536f159c5217f560e97e125b956b5fa8","node_id":"C_kwDOR56yYtoAKDE0ZTg1YWUyNTM2ZjE1OWM1MjE3ZjU2MGU5N2UxMjViOTU2YjVmYTg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/04456543e3ee5651f4af0ddf8107774beccd750e","sha":"04456543e3ee5651f4af0ddf8107774beccd750e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/04456543e3ee5651f4af0ddf8107774beccd750e"}],"sha":"14e85ae2536f159c5217f560e97e125b956b5fa8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/14e85ae2536f159c5217f560e97e125b956b5fa8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/04456543e3ee5651f4af0ddf8107774beccd750e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-10T16:46:37Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-10T16:46:37Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(recording): store object deltas as their scalar leaves (#129)\n\n* feat(recording): store object deltas as their scalar leaves\n\nnavigation.attitude was never recorded. Values are routed by JS type, and\nobjects fell through to null -- so {roll, pitch, yaw} was dropped, along\nwith every other object-valued path. It happened silently: no warning, no\ncounter, and the path never appeared in getPaths, so the only way to find\nout was to go looking for data that had never been there. A user asked\nafter running the plugin for weeks.\n\nObjects other than navigation.position now record each scalar leaf as its\nown dotted path, so attitude arrives as navigation.attitude.roll, .pitch\nand .yaw. They are ordinary scalar paths, so there is no schema change and\ndedup, retention, sampling, filtering and both history APIs apply to them\nunchanged.\n\nnavigation.position keeps its dedicated table. signalk_position is keyed\non ts+context with no path column, and letting other lat/lon objects in\nwould interleave anchor and waypoint fixes with the vessel track. Those\nflatten instead, so an anchor''s coordinates are still recorded -- under\ntheir own path, where they cannot be mistaken for the track.\n\nLeaves are filtered and throttled on the LEAF path, not the parent. The\nleaves are what the history API exposes, so excluding\nnavigation.attitude.yaw has to actually exclude it; and gating the parent\nas well would consume a throttle slot the leaves then wait out again,\nhalving the effective rate.\n\nOne level deep, deliberately -- a recursive walk would write out whole\nnotification and resource payloads nobody asked to record. Arrays are\nexcluded too: their indices are not stable identities, so foo.0 would mean\na different thing from one delta to the next.\n\nWhat still cannot be stored is now reported rather than dropped in\nsilence: each such path is logged once via app.debug and counted on\n/api/status. Silent loss was the underlying bug here, and it survives this\nchange for arrays and deep nesting.\n\nCloses #128\n\n* fix(recording): cap unstorable-path tracking and drop its hot-path log\n\nTwo real defects in the tracking added alongside flattening, both found in\nreview.\n\nThe Set was unbounded. My comment claimed it ''bounds memory'', which is only\ntrue if the path vocabulary is finite -- and it is not. Paths embed instance\nidentifiers (watermaker.0.* appears 8 times in a real database here) and\nnotifications embed per-vessel ones, so an unstorable shape on a busy AIS\nstream would grow the Set for the lifetime of the process. Now hard capped,\nwith the count exposed as a lower bound and a truncated flag saying so\nrather than letting the number read as exact.\n\nIt also called app.debug from the per-delta handler. That path can carry\n100+ values/sec, and app.debug builds its message string whether or not\ndebug is enabled -- the repo''s own hot-path rule. Removed: the diagnostic\nbelongs on /api/status anyway, which is where a user will actually see it.\nThis class of loss went unnoticed precisely because it was only ever\nvisible in a log nobody had switched on.\n\nExtracted as UnstorableTracker so the cap is testable rather than buried in\nthe plugin closure, with coverage for the boundary (at the cap is not yet\ntruncated), for dedup continuing to work past it, and for a full reset on\nclear.\n\nRefs #128\n\n* fix(recording): count unstorable paths before the filter and throttle gates\n\nA null route -- an array, or anything else with no representation -- was\nrecorded only after shouldRecord() and isThrottled() had both passed. So a\nuser who filtered or throttled that path got the old behaviour back: the\nvalue was dropped and nothing counted it, which is precisely the case the\ntracking exists to surface.\n\nRecord it immediately after routing and return. The gates now apply only to\nvalues that can actually be written.\n\nAlso strengthen the coordinate test to assert \"flatten\" rather than merely\n\"not position\" -- the weaker form would still pass if those objects\nregressed to being dropped outright.\n\nBoth found in review.\n\nRefs #128\n\n* fix(recording): stop double-counting unstorable objects, restore design.length\n\nTwo defects found auditing the flattening change.\n\nAn object whose leaves are ALL unstorable was reported twice: once per\nskipped leaf, and again for the parent path. That inflates the count, and\nworse, burns a second slot against the tracking cap -- measured at 16 of 50\nslots consumed by redundant parents on a stream of per-vessel wrapper\nobjects, so the cap is reached sooner and genuinely distinct paths are\nhidden. Report the leaves only; they already name the problem. An object\nwith no storable leaf is still visible through them, and the only silent\ncase left is an empty object, which carries no data to lose.\n\ndesign.length is an OBJECT in Signal K ({overall, hull}), so once objects\nflatten it is recorded as design.length.overall -- but restore''s allowlist\nmatches paths exactly, so it queried a bare design.length that no longer\nexists and silently restored no vessel length. Confirmed against a live\ndatabase: design.beam is present, design.length is absent, because it was\nbeing dropped as an object even before this change. Both spellings are now\nlisted, the leaf for new data and the bare path for databases recorded by a\nsource that emitted it as a plain number.\n\nRefs #128\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"f3b8cc1ddb1a5502f0b47b2bb68bb2388da076e2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/f3b8cc1ddb1a5502f0b47b2bb68bb2388da076e2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/04456543e3ee5651f4af0ddf8107774beccd750e","verification":{"payload":"tree f3b8cc1ddb1a5502f0b47b2bb68bb2388da076e2\nparent b718e0319fbe9139ba655777f7731c359d85d08a\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1786380397 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786380397 +1200\n\nfeat(recording): store object deltas as their scalar leaves (#129)\n\n* feat(recording): store object deltas as their scalar leaves\n\nnavigation.attitude was never recorded. Values are routed by JS type, and\nobjects fell through to null -- so {roll, pitch, yaw} was dropped, along\nwith every other object-valued path. It happened silently: no warning, no\ncounter, and the path never appeared in getPaths, so the only way to find\nout was to go looking for data that had never been there. A user asked\nafter running the plugin for weeks.\n\nObjects other than navigation.position now record each scalar leaf as its\nown dotted path, so attitude arrives as navigation.attitude.roll, .pitch\nand .yaw. They are ordinary scalar paths, so there is no schema change and\ndedup, retention, sampling, filtering and both history APIs apply to them\nunchanged.\n\nnavigation.position keeps its dedicated table. signalk_position is keyed\non ts+context with no path column, and letting other lat/lon objects in\nwould interleave anchor and waypoint fixes with the vessel track. Those\nflatten instead, so an anchor''s coordinates are still recorded -- under\ntheir own path, where they cannot be mistaken for the track.\n\nLeaves are filtered and throttled on the LEAF path, not the parent. The\nleaves are what the history API exposes, so excluding\nnavigation.attitude.yaw has to actually exclude it; and gating the parent\nas well would consume a throttle slot the leaves then wait out again,\nhalving the effective rate.\n\nOne level deep, deliberately -- a recursive walk would write out whole\nnotification and resource payloads nobody asked to record. Arrays are\nexcluded too: their indices are not stable identities, so foo.0 would mean\na different thing from one delta to the next.\n\nWhat still cannot be stored is now reported rather than dropped in\nsilence: each such path is logged once via app.debug and counted on\n/api/status. Silent loss was the underlying bug here, and it survives this\nchange for arrays and deep nesting.\n\nCloses #128\n\n* fix(recording): cap unstorable-path tracking and drop its hot-path log\n\nTwo real defects in the tracking added alongside flattening, both found in\nreview.\n\nThe Set was unbounded. My comment claimed it ''bounds memory'', which is only\ntrue if the path vocabulary is finite -- and it is not. Paths embed instance\nidentifiers (watermaker.0.* appears 8 times in a real database here) and\nnotifications embed per-vessel ones, so an unstorable shape on a busy AIS\nstream would grow the Set for the lifetime of the process. Now hard capped,\nwith the count exposed as a lower bound and a truncated flag saying so\nrather than letting the number read as exact.\n\nIt also called app.debug from the per-delta handler. That path can carry\n100+ values/sec, and app.debug builds its message string whether or not\ndebug is enabled -- the repo''s own hot-path rule. Removed: the diagnostic\nbelongs on /api/status anyway, which is where a user will actually see it.\nThis class of loss went unnoticed precisely because it was only ever\nvisible in a log nobody had switched on.\n\nExtracted as UnstorableTracker so the cap is testable rather than buried in\nthe plugin closure, with coverage for the boundary (at the cap is not yet\ntruncated), for dedup continuing to work past it, and for a full reset on\nclear.\n\nRefs #128\n\n* fix(recording): count unstorable paths before the filter and throttle gates\n\nA null route -- an array, or anything else with no representation -- was\nrecorded only after shouldRecord() and isThrottled() had both passed. So a\nuser who filtered or throttled that path got the old behaviour back: the\nvalue was dropped and nothing counted it, which is precisely the case the\ntracking exists to surface.\n\nRecord it immediately after routing and return. The gates now apply only to\nvalues that can actually be written.\n\nAlso strengthen the coordinate test to assert \"flatten\" rather than merely\n\"not position\" -- the weaker form would still pass if those objects\nregressed to being dropped outright.\n\nBoth found in review.\n\nRefs #128\n\n* fix(recording): stop double-counting unstorable objects, restore design.length\n\nTwo defects found auditing the flattening change.\n\nAn object whose leaves are ALL unstorable was reported twice: once per\nskipped leaf, and again for the parent path. That inflates the count, and\nworse, burns a second slot against the tracking cap -- measured at 16 of 50\nslots consumed by redundant parents on a stream of per-vessel wrapper\nobjects, so the cap is reached sooner and genuinely distinct paths are\nhidden. Report the leaves only; they already name the problem. An object\nwith no storable leaf is still visible through them, and the only silent\ncase left is an empty object, which carries no data to lose.\n\ndesign.length is an OBJECT in Signal K ({overall, hull}), so once objects\nflatten it is recorded as design.length.overall -- but restore''s allowlist\nmatches paths exactly, so it queried a bare design.length that no longer\nexists and silently restored no vessel length. Confirmed against a live\ndatabase: design.beam is present, design.length is absent, because it was\nbeing dropped as an object even before this change. Both spellings are now\nlisted, the leaf for new data and the bare path for databases recorded by a\nsource that emitted it as a plain number.\n\nRefs #128\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqegBtCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA26AQAGwOVLJgjiyE3F0pRh9Qq9dF\nJv9tn9APLhYiOAbU5drOmOnzckvaruLZJ8PmI6o9htC2IeE5Qo+GirLwqv8z/YQ8\n9HlEsCGDZdvrZJRG01Ai4gmnD2hmF+YgU50cTV7PGzMZB1CRTUYRt8wKf039zWaY\n5asjWev1zjTmmAKMJtnOxK7UXM21IZByb51I6cvXl2CP9wdRCHNeDy3f9Xy6aCo2\ny+MCJblAGKsCIC8MUpoznm1QYPjrsoMp0UWM8im6I2Pe32ffDT+MBNHjuLA7ksJj\n6XIXONvVb8VPEznkzjHtCl9mMNemr04uTUtywBdCbAUZPA/CJT7K6q6tdrzCv9nG\nXn1Z4v4ZUJc+LNtnKVFdNboyypKT3wadzjQx40Nio9vlw2OqvPyXiHxTeaIdlViy\nDIJ2OJVeJ6z2fjWNh4DwwHldzeOBZ+m2pfWOpXl9pRaD9H35bDg7S3vXB3lEh1ht\ne/3BtfQXu+07ETrxkZMy/8aG91QaimGDlDvMX