ceived_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/faa643a513239f6a0bb23e4ce1086e9abfdc8f88","node_id":"C_kwDOR56yYtoAKGZhYTY0M2E1MTMyMzlmNmEwYmIyM2U0Y2UxMDg2ZTlhYmZkYzhmODg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/b80ae15d6576688220fce431f23d2bbd54f758f1","sha":"b80ae15d6576688220fce431f23d2bbd54f758f1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/b80ae15d6576688220fce431f23d2bbd54f758f1"}],"sha":"faa643a513239f6a0bb23e4ce1086e9abfdc8f88","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/faa643a513239f6a0bb23e4ce1086e9abfdc8f88"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/b80ae15d6576688220fce431f23d2bbd54f758f1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-07T18:59:10Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-07T18:59:10Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(console): serve QuestDB''s web console as an embedded webapp (#113)\n\n* feat(console): serve QuestDB''s web console as an embedded webapp\n\nQuestDB''s own console is the best tool for inspecting recorded data, and it is\neffectively unreachable: it lives on QuestDB''s HTTP port, bound to loopback\nunless the operator enables \"Bind to 0.0.0.0\". Reaching it from another device\non the boat therefore meant publishing an UNAUTHENTICATED SQL endpoint on the\nnetwork.\n\nThis proxies it at /plugins/signalk-questdb/console/ and adds the\nsignalk-embeddable-webapp keyword, so the console renders inside the Signal K\nadmin UI with the navigation panel still in place — and behind a Signal K admin\nsession rather than no authentication at all.\n\nAdmin-only comes from signalk-server: routes registered directly on a plugin\nrouter default to admin, and only router.access(...) downgrades them. The mount\ndeliberately does not call it, and both the code comment and CLAUDE.md say why,\nbecause the console is a full SQL client that can drop tables.\n\nEnabled by default. Unlike restoreOnStart or exposeToContainers, which change\nwhat the plugin does on its own, the console only ever does what an\nauthenticated admin types — gating it behind a toggle would not stop that\nperson, only hide the tool from everyone who wanted to run a SELECT. It can\nstill be switched off, which removes the route rather than leaving it to refuse.\n\nThe proxy confines requests to the resolved QuestDB origin so a crafted path\ncannot re-target it, and strips the Signal K session cookie and Authorization\nheader, which QuestDB has no use for.\n\nNo bundle rewriting is needed: the console requests assets and its API\nrelatively, verified end to end through the path prefix.\n\n* fix(console): stop the HEAD probe reporting a working console as broken\n\nFound by looking at the running server: the webapp showed \"console\nunavailable\" while the console itself loaded fine in the frame beside it.\n\nThe shim probed the route with HEAD to decide which to show. QuestDB answers\nHEAD with 405 — and sends `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` WITH a body, which is\nillegal for a HEAD response. Piping that through left the client waiting for\nchunks that never legally arrive, so the connection died with an empty reply\nand the probe failed every time.\n\nFixed on both sides. The shim now probes with GET, matching what the iframe\nactually issues. The proxy terminates HEAD responses itself and discards the\nupstream body, so any client sending HEAD gets a clean 405 instead of a hang.\n\nAlso fixes the proxy in external-QuestDB mode: it keyed off `questdbEndpoints`,\nwhich is only populated on the managed-container path, so a non-managed setup\nwould have got 503 forever even though questdbHttpBaseUrl() already falls back\nto the configured host and port.\n\nThe connection-refused test no longer assumes port 1 is free; it binds an\nephemeral port and releases it, so refusal is guaranteed rather than likely.\n\n* fix(console): filter response headers and narrow what the console may do\n\nReview of #113.\n\nHop-by-hop filtering was applied to the request only, and used a fixed\nblocklist. RFC 7230 §6.1 lets `Connection` nominate ADDITIONAL single-hop\nheaders, so those were forwarded; and the upstream''s own hop-by-hop headers\nwere relayed to the browser, letting QuestDB''s framing decisions override the\nones Node makes for the client''s connection. Both directions now strip the\nfixed set plus whatever `Connection` names.\n\nThe review also flagged that serving the console under the Signal K origin\nlets its scripts ride the admin''s session. That is real — Signal K''s auth\ncookie is httpOnly so script cannot read it, but it is still attached to\nsame-origin requests. The suggested fix, a separate origin, is not something a\nplugin can mint, so this narrows what the console may do instead:\n`form-action`, `base-uri` and `frame-ancestors` all pinned to ''self''.\n\nDeliberately NOT `connect-src ''self''`, though it reads safer: the console''s SQL\neditor is Monaco, loaded via a jsdelivr path baked into QuestDB''s bundle.\nStatic analysis could not settle whether that path is taken, and blanking out\nthe query editor would be a worse outcome than the risk it mitigates. A test\npins the omission so tightening it later is a deliberate decision rather than\nan accident, and the comment records what to re-check first.\n\nVerified against the real console: headers applied, HTML, the 3MB bundle and\n/exec all still working.\n\n* fix(console): do not let QuestDB set cookies on the Signal K origin\n\nReview of #113. The proxy stripped `cookie` from requests but relayed\n`Set-Cookie` back unchanged — an asymmetry I missed.\n\nBecause the console is served under the Signal K origin, the browser applies\nany proxied `Set-Cookie` to it, so a misconfigured or compromised QuestDB could\nwrite a `Path=/` cookie that collides with Signal K''s own session handling.\nNothing is lost by dropping it: with request cookies already stripped, no\nQuestDB cookie could ever have completed a round trip.\n\nTest verified in both directions — it fails with the filter removed.\n\n* test(console): make the raw-socket header test sandbox-safe\n\nCaught by running the plugin registry''s own gate locally rather than trusting\n`npm test`: the new Connection-nominated-header test passed normally but hung\nthe whole suite under `firejail --net=none`, which would have cost the 30\npoints #114 just recovered.\n\nTwo causes, both in the test:\n\n`http.request` was given no `host`, so it defaulted to `localhost`. That\nresolves differently inside the sandbox and failed with ECONNREFUSED — while\n`fetch`, used by every other test here, was unaffected. The server listens on\n127.0.0.1, so name it.\n\nThe request also sent `connection: keep-alive`, which held the socket open so\n`server.close()` never completed and the run stalled to the timeout. It now\nsends `connection: close` — still nominating the header this test is about —\nand closes any lingering connection during teardown.\n\nVerified with the registry''s exact invocation:\n`firejail --net=none --read-only=/home -- timeout 60s npm test` exits 0 with\n317 passing.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"5b184bdbdd85e4348e6a87d2f24166b2321d160e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/5b184bdbdd85e4348e6a87d2f24166b2321d160e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/b80ae15d6576688220fce431f23d2bbd54f758f1","verification":{"payload":"tree 5b184bdbdd85e4348e6a87d2f24166b2321d160e\nparent ec5a02542bd993e72fb8cf41fae4d96f70b5069e\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1786129150 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786129150 +1200\n\nfeat(console): serve QuestDB''s web console as an embedded webapp (#113)\n\n* feat(console): serve QuestDB''s web console as an embedded webapp\n\nQuestDB''s own console is the best tool for inspecting recorded data, and it is\neffectively unreachable: it lives on QuestDB''s HTTP port, bound to loopback\nunless the operator enables \"Bind to 0.0.0.0\". Reaching it from another device\non the boat therefore meant publishing an UNAUTHENTICATED SQL endpoint on the\nnetwork.\n\nThis proxies it at /plugins/signalk-questdb/console/ and adds the\nsignalk-embeddable-webapp keyword, so the console renders inside the Signal K\nadmin UI with the navigation panel still in place — and behind a Signal K admin\nsession rather than no authentication at all.\n\nAdmin-only comes from signalk-server: routes registered directly on a plugin\nrouter default to admin, and only router.access(...) downgrades them. The mount\ndeliberately does not call it, and both the code comment and CLAUDE.md say why,\nbecause the console is a full SQL client that can drop tables.\n\nEnabled by default. Unlike restoreOnStart or exposeToContainers, which change\nwhat the plugin does on its own, the console only ever does what an\nauthenticated admin types — gating it behind a toggle would not stop that\nperson, only hide the tool from everyone who wanted to run a SELECT. It can\nstill be switched off, which removes the route rather than leaving it to refuse.\n\nThe proxy confines requests to the resolved QuestDB origin so a crafted path\ncannot re-target it, and strips the Signal K session cookie and Authorization\nheader, which QuestDB has no use for.\n\nNo bundle rewriting is needed: the console requests assets and its API\nrelatively, verified end to end through the path prefix.\n\n* fix(console): stop the HEAD probe reporting a working console as broken\n\nFound by looking at the running server: the webapp showed \"console\nunavailable\" while the console itself loaded fine in the frame beside it.\n\nThe shim probed the route with HEAD to decide which to show. QuestDB answers\nHEAD with 405 — and sends `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` WITH a body, which is\nillegal for a HEAD response. Piping that through left the client waiting for\nchunks that never legally arrive, so the connection died with an empty reply\nand the probe failed every time.\n\nFixed on both sides. The shim now probes with GET, matching what the iframe\nactually issues. The proxy terminates HEAD responses itself and discards the\nupstream body, so any client sending HEAD gets a clean 405 instead of a hang.\n\nAlso fixes the proxy in external-QuestDB mode: it keyed off `questdbEndpoints`,\nwhich is only populated on the managed-container path, so a non-managed setup\nwould have got 503 forever even though questdbHttpBaseUrl() already falls back\nto the configured host and port.\n\nThe connection-refused test no longer assumes port 1 is free; it binds an\nephemeral port and releases it, so refusal is guaranteed rather than likely.\n\n* fix(console): filter response headers and narrow what the console may do\n\nReview of #113.\n\nHop-by-hop filtering was applied to the request only, and used a fixed\nblocklist. RFC 7230 §6.1 lets `Connection` nominate ADDITIONAL single-hop\nheaders, so those were forwarded; and the upstream''s own hop-by-hop headers\nwere relayed to the browser, letting QuestDB''s framing decisions override the\nones Node makes for the client''s connection. Both directions now strip the\nfixed set plus whatever `Connection` names.\n\nThe review also flagged that serving the console under the Signal K origin\nlets its scripts ride the admin''s session. That is real — Signal K''s auth\ncookie is httpOnly so script cannot read it, but it is still attached to\nsame-origin requests. The suggested fix, a separate origin, is not something a\nplugin can mint, so this narrows what the console may do instead:\n`form-action`, `base-uri` and `frame-ancestors` all pinned to ''self''.\n\nDeliberately NOT `connect-src ''self''`, though it reads safer: the console''s SQL\neditor is Monaco, loaded via a jsdelivr path baked into QuestDB''s bundle.\nStatic analysis could not settle whether that path is taken, and blanking out\nthe query editor would be a worse outcome than the risk it mitigates. A test\npins the omission so tightening it later is a deliberate decision rather than\nan accident, and the comment records what to re-check first.\n\nVerified against the real console: headers applied, HTML, the 3MB bundle and\n/exec all still working.\n\n* fix(console): do not let QuestDB set cookies on the Signal K origin\n\nReview of #113. The proxy stripped `cookie` from requests but relayed\n`Set-Cookie` back unchanged — an asymmetry I missed.\n\nBecause the console is served under the Signal K origin, the browser applies\nany proxied `Set-Cookie` to it, so a misconfigured or compromised QuestDB could\nwrite a `Path=/` cookie that collides with Signal K''s own session handling.\nNothing is lost by dropping it: with request cookies already stripped, no\nQuestDB cookie could ever have completed a round trip.\n\nTest verified in both directions — it fails with the filter removed.\n\n* test(console): make the raw-socket header test sandbox-safe\n\nCaught by running the plugin registry''s own gate locally rather than trusting\n`npm test`: the new Connection-nominated-header test passed normally but hung\nthe whole suite under `firejail --net=none`, which would have cost the 30\npoints #114 just recovered.\n\nTwo causes, both in the test:\n\n`http.request` was given no `host`, so it defaulted to `localhost`. That\nresolves differently inside the sandbox and failed with ECONNREFUSED — while\n`fetch`, used by every other test here, was unaffected. The server listens on\n127.0.0.1, so name it.\n\nThe request also sent `connection: keep-alive`, which held the socket open so\n`server.close()` never completed and the run stalled to the timeout. It now\nsends `connection: close` — still nominating the header this test is about —\nand closes any lingering connection during teardown.\n\nVerified with the registry''s exact invocation:\n`firejail --net=none --read-only=/home -- timeout 60s npm test` exits 0 with\n317 passing.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqdir+CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAnxIQAE8CbuT7QGWto4ch+yznujgl\n7ochHtHUxxh+OSDjre0k8zYoo+/Vd9p4IMVdilLrJ1ScDXLZw4xPnKUhg45wS4XX\nxdZ4X1U9xlKv4zXGszwq6qR3EsCkdQCflHJ+6fx2AoL1lborOkJ5wHu2OrMntn9s\nJr6Urgyc+eCuKiX2GcX3+GPOWGz/hzuBmtw7hhdanZXjr7mpsG2nGAT4VBhbzIyB\nEfDWysIkfWX/XX6bzLKPbKKDI1LWXauwa0WT8if1742kSmoIyo9XPhgaSxVkdsm4\nexXOFiRsYS3NjcrNB4wjD4dxfosJ+tZhCc9JIMD/62pF7rBljWKa+CI4QjM44L3b\nuXdeXbiCSohGBlGc7BIjUHW4ub+TlnWevxeYT0w7uqHQF1QBsUs2yJI/mWJQTPu0\nBonX+W80698r13rp6FB9x1YXdckzzCZQKSaBqhtU2jBNoLTbhCvgKrgkz6qCYUnQ\n6tuIBC/vQ2XwK3uvLm80l6k846CwhXazM/PnutLFODQRGt57aE4ikUG7rKjAIE+A\n2WSdQTkXVYVkkafFbCnStZO9wUPyJtu9dVZiuq5+l8opJpTmva9nfnKWTf+oyqxg\nLDl74/Y6tBgysJ32g9Yfp8CzvsssCf3XERcNVdmXHM7ndCE5Fw+iawD/9cFQc4DA\n5MsEi5LwS/8t1nP1X8h1\n=sG89\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-07T18:59:15Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/b80ae15d6576688220fce431f23d2bbd54f758f1","node_id":"C_kwDOR56yYtoAKGI4MGFlMTVkNjU3NjY4ODIyMGZjZTQzMWYyM2QyYmJkNTRmNzU4ZjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commit/ec5a02542bd993e72fb8cf41fae4d96f70b5069e","sha":"ec5a02542bd993e72fb8cf41fae4d96f70b5069e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/ec5a02542bd993e72fb8cf41fae4d96f70b5069e"}],"sha":"b80ae15d6576688220fce431f23d2bbd54f758f1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/b80ae15d6576688220fce431f23d2bbd54f758f1"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86628605?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dirkwa","id":86628605,"login":"dirkwa","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NjI4NjA1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dirkwa","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/commits/ec5a02542bd993e72fb8cf41fae4d96f70b5069e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-07T18:28:14Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-07T18:28:14Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(test): make the pack manifest read work in a read-only sandbox (#114)\n\nThe plugin registry scores plugins by running `npm test` inside\n`firejail --net=none --read-only=/home`. `npm pack` writes to its cache even\nfor `--dry-run`, so the default cache under $HOME failed with EROFS and all\nthree manifest-based assertions errored — reporting `tests-failing` and costing\n30 points while the suite passed everywhere else.\n\nPoints the cache at a temp dir and adds --offline, so the manifest read needs\nneither a writable home nor the network.\n\nThis is why 1.7.0 and 1.7.1 both scored 70 despite the playback-timer fix in\n#110: that fix was real and did resolve the 60s timeout — the scan now fails in\n6s instead of hanging — but it uncovered this second, unrelated failure\nunderneath it.\n\nVerified by reproducing the registry''s exact invocation locally:\n`firejail --net=none --read-only=/home -- timeout 60s npm test` now exits 0\nwith 312 passing, and fails without this change.\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"11b2e5af5fa5675b4f09b0d1967519455dfe479c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/trees/11b2e5af5fa5675b4f09b0d1967519455dfe479c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dirkwa/signalk-questdb/git/commits/ec5a02542bd993e72fb8cf41fae4d96f70b5069e","verification":{"payload":"tree 11b2e5af5fa5675b4f09b0d1967519455dfe479c\nparent 4da7160face9f03b90fa010a5eec44540e08a27f\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1786127294 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786127294 +1200\n\nfix(test): make the pack manifest read work in a read-only sandbox (#114)\n\nThe plugin registry scores plugins by running `npm test` inside\n`firejail --net=none --read-only=/home`. `npm pack` writes to its cache even\nfor `--dry-run`, so the default cache under $HOME failed with EROFS and all\nthree manifest-based assertions errored — reporting `tests-failing` and costing\n30 points while the suite passed everywhere else.\n\nPoints the cache at a temp dir and adds --offline, so the manifest read needs\nneither a writable home nor the network.\n\nThis is why 1.7.0 and 1.7.1 both scored 70 despite the playback-timer fix in\n#110: that fix was real and did resolve the 60s timeout — the scan now fails in\n6s instead of hanging — but it uncovered this second, unrelated failure\nunderneath it.\n\nVerified by reproducing the registry''s exact invocation locally:\n`firejail --net=none --read-only=/home -- timeout 60s npm test` now exits 0\nwith 312 passing, and fails without this change.\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqdiO+CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAsP4QABf5IWdTv9rPPw7TdqjOxB6